INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5990,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6280825','ECDC definitions and methods for the surveillance of healthcare-associated infections in intensive care units.','Plachouras D, Lepape A, Suetens C.','2018','hais hais helics hais hais hais hais cris helicswin helicswinnet hwn hais hais litres antiseptics damp visibly soiled hais cris nhsn cla cla incriminating nhsn pn1 pn5 pn1 pn5 nhsn nhsn hais hais hais hais 53kb savey mercedes palomar recerca lleida irbll lleida antonella agodi hismayr wiv isp jana prattingerova liberec pille tallinn petra gastmeier kurcz luisa agenzia regione romagna jolanta asembergiene vilnius schmit scicluna msida ppcira roxana serban bucharest stefkovicova dubcek trencin jodie mccoubrey dominique monnet 2_0 aer hcai_icu suetens savey labeeuw helics helics nhsn nhsn pscmanual pcsmanual_current sihler chenoweth zalewski hyzy kooi sax pittet dissel benthem walder cartier clack greeff wolkewitz hieke boshuizen kassteele abeele boo elschahawi dumpis ghita lejko leleu paniara patyi schab raglio szilagyi zietkiewicz zingg lerma palomar lorente gordo jam gatell calatayud agra craven wojcik mccoubrey laurenson kefala sohr geffers astagneau blacky koller palomar szilagyi suetens gastmeier 53kb','On 5 May 2017, the European Centre for Disease Prevention and Control (ECDC) published its updated protocol for surveillance of healthcare-associated infections (HAIs) and prevention indicators in European intensive care units (ICUs) [ 1 ]. The ECDC’s Healthcare-Associated Infections surveillance Network (HAI-Net) has been coordinating European surveillance of HAIs in ICUs since 2008 with participation of 15 countries and 1365 ICUs in 2015 [ 2 ]. The methodology was originally developed by the Hospitals in Europe Link for Infection Control through Surveillance (HELICS) project in 2000–2002 [ 3 ] and adopted by ECDC in 2010. A revision of the first protocol was agreed at consensus expert meetings in 2013–2015. The objective of the ECDC surveillance protocol is the standardisation of case definitions, data collection and reporting procedures for hospitals participating in national or regional surveillance of HAIs in ICUs across Europe in order to facilitate the collection of comparable epidemiological data as a basis for interventions and the communication and exchange of experience of national surveillance systems. The case definitions of HAIs aim to accommodate differences, across Europe, in diagnostic practices such as taking microbiological samples for the diagnosis of HAIs. The minimal requirement for participation in surveillance of HAIs in ICUs is surveillance of bloodstream infections (BSIs) and pneumonia (PN). Reporting of urinary tract infections (UTIs) and catheter-related infections (CRIs) is optional. The data are collected by the national surveillance systems and submitted annually to ECDC. The results are published on the ECDC website [ 2 ]. A software application, HelicsWin.Net, is available free-of-charge for entry of HAI surveillance data ( https://ecdc.europa.eu/en/publications-data/helicswinnet-hwn ). The latest update of the ECDC protocol was triggered by the unexplained, large variation in the prevalence of HAIs among European countries reported by the ECDC point prevalence survey of HAIs and antimicrobial use in European acute care hospitals 2011–2012. Structure and process indicators for infection prevention and control and antimicrobial stewardship (Table 1 ), as well as a new variable on the outcome of the HAI were included in the latest version of the surveillance protocol. These modifications were developed by ECDC and HAI-Net ICU experts and agreed during an HAI-Net ICU meeting in February 2015. Table 1. Structure and process indicators for infection prevention control and antimicrobial stewardship in intensive care units (ICUs) Area Indicator Metric Hand hygiene Alcohol hand rub consumption in ICU during the previous year Litres per 1000 patient-days Staffing Registered nurse hours in ICU Hours per 1000 patient-days Nursing assistant hours in ICU Hours per 1000 patient-days Antimicrobial stewardship Review antimicrobial therapy within 72 h Proportion (%) compliant observations Intubation Endotracheal pressure controlled and/or corrected at least twice a day Proportion (%) compliant observations Oral decontamination using oral antiseptics at least twice a day Proportion (%) compliant observations Position of the patient not supine Proportion (%) compliant observations Central venous catheter Catheter site dressing is not damp, loose or visibly soiled Proportion (%) compliant observations Open in a new tab The ECDC case definitions applied for surveillance of HAIs in European ICUs are presented in Table 1a in the supplementary material. The case definition for BSI addresses both primary and secondary BSIs. A definition for CRI establishes the microbiological criteria for assignment of the catheter as the origin of the BSI. When CRIs are not specifically reported, BSIs for which the same microorganism was cultured from the catheter or when symptoms improved within 48 h after removal of the catheter are reported as BSIs with origin central venous catheter (CVC). In this way, it is possible to assess the burden of BSIs linked to catheters in ICUs with different local practices for microbiological confirmation through culture of the catheter tip or taking blood samples through the catheter for quantitative culture or differential time to positivity. This definition differs from the definition of the National Healthcare Safety Network of the US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC/NHSN) of central line-associated bloodstream infection (CLA-BSI) that includes all primary BSIs with a central line in place for at least 2 days on the date of the BSI [ 4 ]. However, it is possible to calculate CLA-BSI rates with HAI-Net ICU data to perform comparisons with data from the US and other surveillance networks that use this definition. The definitions for CRI and BSI with origin CVC were preferred, as they avoid incriminating the catheter for BSI cases with unidentified origin. Such cases may be the result of translocation or a difficult to confirm source and linking them to a central vascular catheter may lead to overestimation of the incidence and dilute the effect of preventive interventions [ 5 ]. The CRI definition has been applied in studies that assess the effect of prevention efforts [ 6 ]. For pneumonia, the ECDC case definition (based on the 2002 NHSN definition) applies imaging, clinical and microbiological criteria and consists of five levels (PN1–PN5), from PN1 requiring microbiological confirmation from minimally contaminated specimens to PN5 requiring no positive microbiological data. These levels address differences in diagnostic practices across Europe and intend to ensure comprehensiveness and inform the interpretation of the results. The definitions primarily aim to support infection prevention and control but they also encourage microbiological confirmation that may impact clinical management in terms of treatment decisions and especially antimicrobial use. The definition for ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP) differs from the current CDC/NHSN definition of ventilator-associated events (VAE), which was implemented in the USA in 2013 [ 5 ]. HAI-Net maintained the previous definition for pneumonia and VAP, as it was considered more relevant from both clinical and prevention perspectives. The definition is in line with the one used in prevention studies and diagnostic and treatment guidelines and has been used in studies that demonstrate the effect of preventive interventions [ 7 ]. Furthermore, a recent meta-analysis indicated that VAE surveillance does not detect cases of VAP accurately [ 8 ]. A comparison with the current CDC/NHSN definition of VAE and probable VAP [ 4 ] showed discordance for VAP, with agreement in only four of 61 cases identified as VAP by either one or the other definition [ 9 ]. In contrast, a study comparing the ECDC definition with the previous US definition for VAP had demonstrated very high concordance when applying both definitions in the same group of patients [ 10 ]. The updated ECDC protocol for surveillance of HAIs in European ICUs combines outcome indicators (incidence of HAIs, microbiology and outcome) with structure and process indicators for infection prevention and control and antimicrobial stewardship. It aims to provide information that is useful and actionable at hospital, regional and national level both for prevention of HAIs and policymaking. EU/EEA countries are invited to apply the protocol in national surveillance systems and participate in European surveillance fostering an EU-wide collaboration to support the prevention of HAIs. Electronic supplementary material Below is the link to the electronic supplementary material. Supplementary material 1 (DOC 53 kb) (53KB, doc) Acknowledgements Anne Savey, Coordination Center for Healthcare-associated Infection Prevention and Control in South-Eastern France, Lyon, France; Mercedes Palomar, Institut Recerca Biomedica Lleida (IRBLL), Lleida, Spain; Antonella Agodi, University of Catania, Italy; Michael Hismayr, Medical University Vienna, Austria; Karl Mertens, Scientific Institute of Public Health (WIV-ISP), Brussels, Belgium; Jana Prattingerová, Regional Public Health Authority, Liberec, Czech Republic; Pille Märtin, West Tallinn Central Hospital, Estonia; Petra Gastmeier, National Reference Center for Surveillance of Nosocomial Infections, Berlin, Germany; Andrea Kurcz, Ministry of Human Capacities, Budapest, Hungary; Maria Luisa Moro, Agenzia Sanitaria e Sociale Regione Emilia-Romagna, Bologna, Italy; Jolanta Asembergiene, National Cancer Institute, Vilnius, Lithuania; Jean-Claude Schmit, Health Directorate, Luxembourg; Elizabeth Anne Scicluna, Infection Control Unit, Mater Dei Hospital, Msida, Malta; Paulo André Fernandes, Program for Prevention and Control of Infections and Resistance to Antimicrobials (PPCIRA), Lisbon, Portugal; Roxana Serban, Regional Centre of Public Health, Bucharest, Romania; Maria Štefkovičová, Alexander Dubcek University, Trenčín, Slovakia; Jodie McCoubrey, Health Protection Scotland, Glasgow, UK; Dominique L. Monnet, European Centre for Disease Prevention and Control, Stockholm, Sweden Compliance with ethical standards Conflicts of interest The authors declare that they have no conflict of interest. Footnotes The original version of this article was revised due to a retrospective Open Access order. Change history 9/17/2018 The article ECDC definitions and methods for the surveillance of healthcare‑associated infections in intensive care units References 1. European Centre for Disease Prevention and Control (2017) Surveillance of healthcare-associated infections and prevention indicators in European intensive care units. ECDC, Stockholm. https://ecdc.europa.eu/sites/portal/files/documents/HAI-Net-ICU-protocol-v2.2_0.pdf . Accessed 12 Feb 2018 2. European Centre for Disease Prevention and Control (2016) Healthcare-associated infections acquired in intensive care units. ECDC, Stockholm. https://ecdc.europa.eu/sites/portal/files/documents/AER-HCAI_ICU.pdf . Accessed 12 Feb 2018 3. Suetens C, Savey A, Labeeuw J, Morales I, HELICS-ICU The ICU-HELICS programme: towards European surveillance of hospital-acquired infections in intensive care units. Euro Surveill. 2002;7:127–128. doi: 10.2807/esm.07.09.00359-en. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (2018) National Healthcare Safety Network (NHSN) Patient Safety Component Manual. CDC, Atlanta. https://www.cdc.gov/nhsn/pdfs/pscmanual/pcsmanual_current.pdf . Accessed 12 Feb 2018 5. Sihler KC, Chenoweth C, Zalewski C, Wahl W, Hyzy R, Napolitano LM. Catheter-related vs. catheter-associated blood stream infections in the intensive care unit: incidence, microbiology, and implications. Surg Infect (Larchmt) 2010;11:529–534. doi: 10.1089/sur.2009.084. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. van der Kooi T, Sax H, Pittet D, van Dissel J, van Benthem B, Walder B, Cartier V, Clack L, de Greeff S, Wolkewitz M, Hieke S, Boshuizen H, van de Kassteele J, Van den Abeele A, Boo TW, Diab-Elschahawi M, Dumpis U, Ghita C, FitzGerald S, Lejko T, Leleu K, Martinez MP, Paniara O, Patyi M, Schab P, Raglio A, Szilagyi E, Zietkiewicz M, Wu AW, Grundmann H, Zingg W, PROHIBIT consortium Prevention of hospital infections by intervention and training (PROHIBIT): results of a pan-European cluster-randomized multicentre study to reduce central venous catheter-related bloodstream infections. Intensive Care Med. 2018;44:48–60. doi: 10.1007/s00134-017-5007-6. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Alvarez-Lerma F, Palomar-Martinez M, Sanchez-Garcia M, Martinez-Alonso M, Alvarez-Rodriguez J, Lorente L, Arias-Rivera S, Garcia R, Gordo F, Anon JM, Jam-Gatell R, Vazquez-Calatayud M, Agra Y. Prevention of ventilator-associated pneumonia: the multimodal approach of the Spanish ICU “Pneumonia Zero” Program. Crit Care Med. 2018;46:181–188. doi: 10.1097/CCM.0000000000002736. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Fan Y, Gao F, Wu Y, Zhang J, Zhu M, Xiong L. Does ventilator-associated event surveillance detect ventilator-associated pneumonia in intensive care units? A systematic review and meta-analysis. Crit Care. 2016;20:338. doi: 10.1186/s13054-016-1506-z. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Craven TH, Wojcik G, McCoubrey J, Brooks O, Grant E, Reilly J, Laurenson IF, Kefala K, Walsh TS. Lack of concordance between ECDC and CDC systems for surveillance of ventilator associated pneumonia. Intensive Care Med. 2018;44:265–266. doi: 10.1007/s00134-017-4993-8. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Hansen S, Sohr D, Geffers C, Astagneau P, Blacky A, Koller W, Morales I, Moro ML, Palomar M, Szilagyi E, Suetens C, Gastmeier P. Concordance between European and US case definitions of healthcare-associated infections. Antimicrob Resist Infect Control. 2012;1:28. doi: 10.1186/2047-2994-1-28. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Supplementary material 1 (DOC 53 kb) (53KB, doc)',7,'2025-10-15 11:45:06','2025-10-15 11:45:06');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5991,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10727539','Circle packing charts generated by ChatGPT to identify the characteristics of articles by anesthesiology authors in 2022: Bibliometric analysis.','Ho SY, Chien TW, Chou W.','2023','t100if sankey showa t100if sankey sankey bibliographical bibliographical bibliographical bylines coauthor t100if k534 coword coword sna sna install jupyter notebook circlize circlize k535 customize regenerating circlepacker nest subsubgroup pathstring bylines eqs t100if t100if coword t100if t100if ibp ibp ibp clicked k535 sankey t100if t100if t100if k535 k536 circlepacker customizing customize showa t100if t100if hironobu ueshima hiroshi otake holger eltzschig t100if ckc ckc ibp t100if t100if ibp ibp t100if t100if dominates t100if balls t100if sankey t100if sankey t100if sankey t100if t100if k537 showa t100if t100if treemaps sunburst circlepacker directories taxonomies dplyr tidyr preprocess bibliographical bibliographical sankey bibliographical arguable disputable dependability ghatgpt dominates dominates enago enago syc twc twc chieh tsair willy e34511 e34511 e34511 ibp sna e34511 chieh t20317 tsair smile chimei opneai e223312 flafi lablab sigchi landweber icicle reymond cheminform cocitation holten adjacency 28anesthesiology 5baffiliation 5bdate 5bdate asahq karaca vapor 5bmesh 28anesthesiology 5baffiliation e11567 kano e11015 sekercioglu coauthor campiteli kinouchi lindsey cjal baltim e32609 ascendancy acronyms chord baltim e32369 jcj installing hopr baltim e30674 sankey baltim e31144 healthup html100 anesthsi100 dcxgx5wxup4 t100if healthup anestharticle youtu scvy5sqpena healthup anesthmesh2 healthup anesthmesh3 healthup anesthmesh ibp healthup annes23ibp healthup anesthaif noorden lariviere gingras theorization motomura okin chronotropic incompetence fabsitz selby brodie pami peccora yazdi acetylomics tufte envisioning heer shneiderman blumenschein pomerenke reordering eurographics eurovis wickham tidyverse wickham grolemund tidy healthup raschonline cbp cbp circlepacking e34511 e34511 e34511','Abstract Background: The ChatGPT (Open AI, San Francisco, CA), denoted by the Chat Generative Pretrained Transformer, has been a hot topic for discussion over the past few months. A verification of whether the code for drawing circle packing charts (CPCs) with R can be generated by ChatGPT and used to identify characteristics of articles by anesthesiology authors is needed. This study aimed to provide insights into article characteristics in the field of anesthesiology and to highlight the potential of ChatGPT for data visualization techniques (e.g., CPCs) in bibliometric analysis. Methods: A total of 23,012 articles were indexed in PubMed in 2022 by authors in the field of anesthesiology. The code for drawing CPCs with R was generated by ChatGPT and then modified by the authors to identify the characteristics of articles in 2 forms: 23,012 and 100 top-impact factors in journals (T100IF). Using CPCs and 3 other visualizations—network charts, impact beam plots, and Sankey diagrams—we were able to display article features commonly used in bibliometric analysis. The author-weighted scheme and absolute advantage coefficient were used to assess dominant entities, such as countries, institutes, authors, and themes (defined by PubMed and MeSH terms). Results: Our findings indicate that: further modifications should be made to the code generated by ChatGPT for drawing CPCs in R; publications in the field of anesthesiology are dominated by China, followed by the United States and Japan; Capital Medical University (China) and Showa University Hospital (Japan) dominate research institutes in terms of publications and IF, respectively; and COVID-19 is the most frequently reported theme in T100IF, accounting for 29%. Conclusions: No such articles with CPCs regarding bibliometrics have ever been found in PubMed. The code for drawing CPCs with R can be generated by ChatGPT, but further modification is required for implementation in bibliometrics. CPCs should be used in future studies to identify the characteristics of articles in other areas of research rather than limiting them to anesthesiology, as we did in this study. Keywords: absolute advantage coefficient, author-weighted scheme, ChatGPT, circle packing charts, impact beam plot, network chart, Sankey diagram Key points. Circle packing charts (CPCs) have never been applied to bibliometric analysis before, especially when the code generated by ChatGPT is used. The article characteristics of anesthesiology were reported using the CPC with code in R deposited in Supplemental Digital Contents for future researchers to replicate. The application of CPCs with 3 other visualizations, such as network charts, impact beam plots, and Sankey diagrams, to bibliographical studies is modern and unique. 1. Introduction ChatGPT (Chat Generative Pretrained Transformer) is a state-of-the-art language model developed by open artificial intelligence (OpenAI) that generates human-like text in response to a given prompt or context. [ 1 , 2 ] Based on a large database of Internet text, ChatGPT is capable of understanding and producing a variety of language styles and topics. [ 3 ] A major application of ChatGPT is text generation, which is poised to revolutionize the way we create content, including academic publications. ChatGPT and other similar models will make discriminating between texts written by humans and those generated by artificial intelligence more difficult. [ 3 ] In contrast, ChatGPT is capable of generating code quickly and with a high degree of accuracy. [ 4 ] By providing us with a template and instructions, ChatGPT can generate code tailored to our specific requirements. An excellent way to speed up the development process and gain an understanding of new coding techniques is by using ChatGPT. The first research question in this study is to determine whether ChatGPT is capable of generating code to create visualizations for bibliographical studies. 1.1. Circle packing charts used for bibliometric analysis Circular packing charts (CPCs) [ 5 ] display hierarchical data in a circular layout, where each circle represents a node, and its size corresponds to a specific variable (e.g., the number of publications and the number of citations). [ 6 – 10 ] In bibliometric analysis, CPCs may be used to display the distribution of publications or citations across different categories, such as countries, institutions, authors, or keywords. By visualizing the hierarchical relationships between these categories, CPCs can be helpful in determining research impact and trends. However, few software packages allow users to draw CPCs on their own. Meanwhile, CPCs have not yet been applied to identify article characteristics on a topic or within a particular discipline in bibliometric research. Following the first research question, ChatGPT is being prompted to generate code in R [ 11 ] to create bibliographical visualizations (e.g., CPCs). 1.2. Characteristics of articles by anesthesiology authors In 2022, over 23,012 articles that were indexed in PubMed were written by anesthesiology authors. [ 12 ] Anesthesiology is the study of anesthesia and anesthetics. [ 13 ] According to the American Society of Anesthesiologists, anesthesiology is defined as “the practice of medicine devoted to the relief of pain and total care of surgical patients before, during, and after surgery.” [ 14 ] The term anesthesia refers to the loss of sensation with or without the loss of consciousness. Anesthesiology is a scientific field that focuses on patient safety before, during, and after any surgical intervention, including relief of pain. [ 15 ] John Snow [ 16 ] wrote the first written document in the field of anesthesia in 1847. The discipline of anesthesia has evolved since that time into a rather broad discipline with a variety of research areas, increasing the number of studies every day. Although 65 articles related to bibliometric analysis were written by anesthesiology authors, [ 17 ] no research used CPCs to identify their characteristics. The second research question in this study is how to highlight the potential of CPCs for data visualization techniques and demonstrate the characteristics of articles by anesthesiologists. 1.3. Challenges encountered in this study Analysis of article characteristics by anesthesiologist authors presented 3 challenges, including the order in which contributions were made by authors in the article bylines; only anesthesiology authors involved in comparing contributions among countries and research institutes; and determining the dominance and strength of contributions to anesthesiology. The authorship-weighted scheme (AWS) [ 18 – 20 ] has been proposed for application to bibliometric metrics as a means of facilitating comparison, although many counting schemes for quantifying coauthor contributions have been proposed. [ 21 – 23 ] Other authors were excluded from the analyses when computing research achievements by extracting anesthesiology authors from articles using computer programming. Furthermore, the dominance strength of contributions can be measured using the absolute advantage coefficient (AAC). [ 24 – 26 ] In this manner, it is possible to analyze the characteristics of articles written by anesthesiologists. 1.4. Study aims This study aimed to provide insights into article characteristics in the field of anesthesiology and to highlight the potential of ChatGPT for data visualization techniques (e.g., CPCs) in bibliometric analysis. Specifically, we achieved 2 objectives: to verify that CPCs can be drawn with R using ChatGPT and to identify the characteristics of articles written by anesthesiology authors. 2. Methods 2.1. Data sources A total of 23,012 articles were indexed in PubMed in 2022 by authors in the field of anesthesiology. Two forms of articles were analyzed: 23,012 and 100 top-impact factors in journals (T100IF); see Supplemental Digital Content, http://links.lww.com/MD/K534 . As this study did not involve the examination or treatment of patients or review of patient records, it was exempt from review and approval by our research ethics committee. 2.2. Three parts involved in this study 2.2.1. Using ChatGPT to generate R code for drawing CPCs. Reasons for drawing CPCs in bibliometrics . The traditional way of depicting the themes derived from coword analysis (or social network analysis) [ 27 , 28 ] was to use network charts or circle network diagrams, as shown in the top and middle panels of Figure 1 . They are unclear when compared to the CPC described in the section Circle packing charts used for bibliometric analysis, CPCs can be used to display one level of data (e.g., only themes are displayed in CPC) or higher hierarchical data (e.g., nested nodes are displayed in CPC), which indicates clusters within brown circles, and their respective elements in white circles, as shown in the bottom panel of Figure 1 (e.g., major elements are shown in CPC). PubMed did not contain any articles containing the keyword CPC. Therefore, CPC was illustrated in this study. We can see that 2 levels include article themes, each with 3 major medical subject headings (MeSH terms in PubMed) shown in the bottom panel of Figure 1 , which is novel and modern more than the traditional network charts shown in the top and middle panels of Figure 1 . Figure 1. Open in a new tab Comparison of 3 presentations with coword analysis using SNA. (note. nine themes are shown with 3 visual presentations; CPC in the bottom panel includes 2 levels of theme and MeSH term). CPC = circle packing charts; MeSH, medical subject headings; SNA = social network analysis. 2.2.1.1. Generating R code for drawing CPCs via ChatGPT. To produce code in R using ChatGPT, we followed these general steps: Step 1: Install R on our computer by downloading it from the official R website [ 29 ] and following the installation instructions. Step 2: Open an Integrated Development Environment for R. There are several Integrated Development Environments available for R, such as RStudio, [ 30 ] Visual Studio Code with R extension, and Jupyter Notebook. Choose the one that suits our needs. Step 3: Import the necessary packages. In R, we can use packages to extend the functionality of the base language. For the CPC, we need the “circlize” package. To import this package, we can then use the library function (e.g., library[circlize], which will be generated by ChatGPT). Step 4: Prepare the data. For the CPC, we need a data frame with the values to plot. ChatGPT can create a simple data frame with the data.frame function (for more detailed examples, see Supplemental Digital Content 2, http://links.lww.com/MD/K535 ). Other relevant codes for drawing CPC will be generated by ChatGPT, including necessary R packages. Step 5: Customize the plot. We can modify the plot by changing the parameters of the function (e.g., to change the colors, labels, and sizes of the elements in the plot). It is worth noting that any given prompt we used to generate an R code by ChatGPT does not generate a unique answer. Regenerating the response using the exact same prompt yields a different result each time. Furthermore, continuous prompts with adjusted expressions are necessary. For example, “ write code with circlepackeR package in R for the task of circle packing chart, particularly having three nest with group, subgroup, and subsubgroup, including code of data$pathString, library(data.tree), without color and with size=”size” and as. Node(data )” is better than the one “ Write code in R about circle packing chart with an example for me .” 2.2.2. Characteristics of 23,012 articles. On the basis of publications and impact factors (IFs), we applied the simple one-level CPCs to display dominant countries and institutes. In CPCs, only the top 20 countries (or institutes) were displayed. AWS [ 18 – 20 ] (i.e., weighted counts based on authorship and number of authors; corresponding authors are assumed at the last position in article bylines) and the allocation scheme [ 31 ] were used to calculate the weighted publications and IFs for each country (or institute). The AAC to denote the dominance strength is determined by the 3 consecutive numbers of values (e.g., counts in descending order denoted by A1, A2, and A3 in Eqs. 1 to 3). [ 24 – 26 ] A A C = ( R 12 / R 23 ) / ( 1 + [ R 12 / R 23 ] ) , (1) R 12 = A 1 / A 2 , (2) R 23 = A 2 / A 3 , (3) The AAC ranged from 0 to 1.0, representing the strength of dominance for the top one when compared with the next 2 counterparts in publications or IFs. Through the computation of AAC, the dominance strength can be measured by the effect size, with criteria of < 0.5, between 0.5 and 0.7, and not less than 0.7 as the small, medium, and large effect sizes, respectively. [ 32 ] 2.2.3. Characteristics of T100IF. T100IF articles [ 33 ] contain 100 top-IF publications written by authors in the field of anesthesiology. Articles were assigned themes based on MeSH terms using coword analysis. [ 27 , 28 ] Details about the classification of themes refer to previous studies. [ 27 , 28 ] All themes and the top 100 authors in T100IF are shown in CPC. T100IF articles [ 33 ] were shown on an impact beam plot (IBP) [ 34 ] and CPC. [ 35 ] All those articles in IBP could be linked to PubMed once the dot representing an article in IBP was selected and clicked. A multilevel CPC was provided, including country, journal, and PMID (unique identification number in PubMed) in levels 1 to 3. The R code to draw CPCs is shown in Supplemental Digital Content 2, http://links.lww.com/MD/K535 . Finally, the Sankey diagram [ 32 ] was provided for readers to understand the relationship of top article entities (e.g., themes, journals, countries, and institutes) and dominance roles in T100IF. 2.3. Worth-reading articles in T100IF Three articles in T100IF were selected to summarize abstracts via ChatGPT using the prompt: write the summary of the abstract in 100 words as shown below . 2.4. Creating Dashboards on Google Maps All graphs were drawn by author-made modules in Excel (Microsoft Corp). We created pages of HTML used for Google Maps. The method of how to conduct this study is deposited with a PDF file and an MP4 video in Supplemental Digital Contents 2, http://links.lww.com/MD/K535 and Supplemental Digital Contents 3, http://links.lww.com/MD/K536 or the reference. [ 36 ] 3. Results 3.1. Using ChatGPT to generate R code for drawing CPCs CPCs are a data visualization technique used to display hierarchical data. ChatGPT can be used to generate R code for drawing CPCs. R is a programming language and software environment for statistical computing and graphics. CPCs can thus be created in R using the circlepackeR package, which provides a variety of functions for customizing the chart’s appearance; see the demonstrations with an MP4 video. [ 36 ] With ChatGPT, we can create and customize CPCs, including setting color schemes, adjusting circle sizes, and labeling chart elements. For data analysis and presentation, this can simplify the process of creating visually appealing and informative CPCs in 2 one-level and multilevel forms. Note that further modifications should be made to the code generated by ChatGPT for drawing CPCs in R; see those CPCs in the following sections. 3.2. Characteristics of 23,012 articles Publications in the field of anesthesiology are dominated by China, followed by the United States and Japan (Figs. 2 and 3 by count and IF, respectively), with an extremely weak dominance effect (24% = AAC). Capital Medical University (China) and Showa University Hospital (Japan) dominate research institutes in terms of publications and IF, respectively, with medium and large dominance effects (57% and 74% by AAC), as shown in Figures 4 and 5 . Figure 2. Open in a new tab Top 20 countries by count using CPC to display. CPC = circle packing charts. Figure 3. Open in a new tab Top 20 countries by IF using CPC to display. CPC = circle packing charts; IF, impact factor. Figure 4. Open in a new tab Top 20 institutes by count using CPC to display. CPC = circle packing charts. Figure 5. Open in a new tab Top 20 institutes by IF using CPC to display. CPC = circle packing charts; IF, impact factor. 3.3. Characteristics of T100IF COVID-19 was the most frequently reported theme in T100IF, accounting for 29%, followed by cardiac surgical procedures (10%) and analgesics and opioids (9%), with a weak dominance effect (15% = AAC) (Fig. 6 ). Figure 6. Open in a new tab Top 100 authors by IF using CPC to display. CPC = circle packing charts; IF, impact factor. Hironobu Ueshima (Japan) has the highest article IF (695.38) when compared with the following 2 authors: Hiroshi Otake (Japan) and Holger Eltzschig (US), with a weak dominance effect (18% = AAC) (Fig. 7 ). Figure 7. Open in a new tab Top 42 chiefs of MeSH terms in T100IF using CKC to display. CKC = circle packing chart; MeSH, medical subject headings. The IBP is shown in Figure 8 , which includes T100IF articles. The article appears on PubMed immediately after clicking the bubble of interest. Figure 8. Open in a new tab T100IF articles using IBP to display. IBP, impact beam plot. T100IF is dominated by the United States on a 3-level CPC. Figure 9 [ 35 ] shows 5 journals in the US, including Lancet, Chem Soc Rev, Chem Rev, Nat Med, JAMA, and N Engl J Med. This indicates that the CPC contains more information than the traditional network chart, as shown in Figure 1 . Similarly, China at the bottom of Figure 9 contains 2 journals in T100IF, including N Engl J Med. (1) and JAMA (4). Figure 9. Open in a new tab The US dominates the T100IF(note. each white balls are noted by articles). An overview of all relevant entities in T100IF is provided by the Sankey diagram (Fig. 10 ). T100IF is dominated by the 4 entities of COVID-19, JAMA, the US, and Mayo Clinic (US). Sankey diagrams have been illustrated in a previous study. [ 32 ] Figure 10. Open in a new tab Characteristics of T100IF in 2022 using the Sankey diagram(note. 3 levels in CPCs include countries, journals, and articles). CPC = circle packing charts. 3.4. Worth-reading articles in T100IF Three articles in T100IF were selected to summarize abstracts via ChatGPT; see the results generated by CHatGPT in Supplemental Digital Content 4, http://links.lww.com/MD/K537 . 3.5. Online dashboards shown on Google Maps All the QR codes in the graphs are linked to the dashboards. [ 35 , 37 – 41 ] Readers are suggested to examine the displayed dashboards on Google Maps. 4. Discussion 4.1. Principal findings Our findings indicate that: further modifications should be made to the code generated by ChatGPT for drawing CPCs in R; China has the largest number of publications in anesthesiology, followed by the United States and Japan; Capital Medical University (China) and Showa University Hospital (Japan) are the most prolific research institutes in terms of publications and impact, respectively; and COVID-19 is the most frequently reported theme in T100IF, accounting for 29% of all reports. Accordingly, 2 objectives have been achieved: to verify that CPCs can be drawn with R using ChatGPT and to identify the characteristics of articles written by anesthesiology authors. 4.2. Additional information Based on articles published in PubMed only in 2022, China was the leading country in the field of anesthesiology, with a weak dominance effect (24% = AAC) (Figs. 2 and 3 ). This is in contrast to the finding that, based on data from the Science Citation Index Expanded (SCI-E) over a period of 2 decades (1995–2014), the United States has the highest number of publications and citations. The reason might be that publications have gradually increased in China in recent years, [ 42 , 43 ] in compliance with the findings in these studies [ 44 – 46 ] that the number of publications originating from China has surpassed the number of publications originating from the United States. Nonetheless, the US-dominated T100IF articles (Fig. 9 ), are in compliance with the findings of the US-dominated publications in high-impact journals. [ 47 – 50 ] According to another study, [ 51 ] the top 5 countries publishing anesthesiology research were the United States, Japan, Germany, Canada, and France. More than 50% of the published articles were submitted from the United States, based on articles published in Anesthesiology between 1941 and 2010 and in Anesthesia and Analgesia between 1931 and 2010. These studies [ 47 – 51 ] and this study differ in their methodology, principally in the definition of anesthesiology research based on anesthesiology authors and AWS-weighted publications and IFs. [ 18 – 20 ] As the publications are only the work of anesthesiology authors, there appears to be merit in conducting a more sophisticated validation process in this study. Furthermore, circle packing charts, also known as circular treemaps or sunburst charts, are visualizations that display hierarchical data as nested circles. In R, the circlepackeR package provides tools for creating such CPCs. [ 52 ] There are several features in CPCs, including: Ability to display hierarchical data: CPCs allow users to display hierarchical data in a circular layout, making it easy to see how items (or elements) are nested within one another. This feature is especially useful for visualizing data such as file directories, website structures, or biological taxonomies. [ 53 ] Support for interactive exploration: CPCs can be made interactive, allowing users to zoom in and out of the chart to explore different levels of the hierarchy. This feature can help users identify patterns and outliers in the data. [ 54 ] Use of color to encode data: CPCs can use color to encode additional data, such as value or category. This feature can help users quickly identify patterns or trends in the data. [ 55 ] Support for customization: CPCs can be customized in various ways, such as adjusting the size and spacing of the circles or changing the color scheme. This feature allows users to tailor the chart to their specific needs. [ 56 ] Integration with other R packages: CPCs can be easily integrated with other R packages, such as dplyr and tidyr, to manipulate and preprocess data before visualization. [ 57 ] 4.3. Implications and changes There are 3 features with the potential to make possible changes to bibliographical studies in the future: CPCs have never been applied to bibliometric analysis before, especially when the code generated by ChatGPT is used and modified to report the characteristics of articles in future bibliographical studies. The article characteristics of anesthesiology were reported using the CPC in R in 2 one-level and multilevel forms. The R code is provided in the Supplemental Digital Contents and R platform [ 58 ] for future researchers to replicate. The application of CPCs with 3 other visualizations, such as network charts, impact beam plots, and Sankey diagrams, to bibliographical studies is modern and unique. We recommend its use in future bibliometric analyses. 4.4. Limitations and suggestions A number of issues need to be examined in further research. The first concern is that the meta-article data and anesthesiology authors were retrieved from PubMed. It is possible that publications in other major bibliometric databases (e.g., Scopus, PubMed, and Google Scholar, etc.) have been overlooked, and the number of publications has been underestimated. Second, dashboards in figures are displayed on websites. Although HTML-format links are easy to read on the Internet, the interpretation of CPC needs to be cautious, particularly with regard to multilevel CPCs, which are difficult to explain without the availability of dashboards. Third, when calculating the dominant roles based on AWS and AAC, we assume that the first and corresponding authors contribute the most to the articles. The results of this study would be biased if the assumption is not supported. Fourth, PubMed does not define authors in a definitive manner, as did Web of Science. The corresponding authors are assumed to be positioned at the end of the article. It would be arguable and disputable if the assumption were not supported. Fifth, the study employed only ChatGPT and did not compare its usefulness to other tools or techniques when creating CPCs. This can reduce the dependability of the results and the generalizability of the method. Nonetheless, the advancement of ChatGPT (e.g., GhatGPT 4.0 outperforms to ChatGPT 3.5) with R code generation can be anticipated in the nearest future. Finally, it is important to note that although we verified that CPCs can be drawn with R by using ChatGPT, as shown in an MP4 video, [ 36 ] any given prompt does not generate a unique or satisfactory answer. It is necessary to use continuous prompts with adjusted expressions to obtain an R code that can be used to draw the CPC, as described in section 2.2.1.2. 5. Conclusions Bibliometrics-related articles with CPCs were not found in PubMed. It is possible to generate the code for drawing CPCs in R using ChatGPT, but further modification is required to implement it in bibliometrics. Future studies should utilize CPCs to identify the characteristics of articles in other areas of research rather than limiting them to anesthesiology. The question of whether China dominates anesthesiology and the United States dominates high-IF journals in anesthesiology should be investigated further in the future. Acknowledgments We thank Enago ( www.enago.tw ) for the English language review of this manuscript. Author Contributions SYC and TWC initiated the research, collected data, conducted the analysis, and wrote the manuscript. WC contributed to the design of the study and provided critical reviews of the manuscript, and WC and TWC contributed to the interpretation of the results. C