adation in brain neurons. PLoS ONE. 2014;9(5):e97756. doi: 10.1371/journal.pone.0097756. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Supplementary Information. (2.6MB, docx) Data Availability Statement All data will be available on reasonable request from the corresponding author.',26,'2025-10-15 11:46:08','2025-10-15 11:46:08');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(6010,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12077471','Exploring the impact of an interventional approach to resident teaching in anesthesiology clerkships.','Zeeni C, Ibrahim H, Zgheib NK, Saliba E, Moukarzel M, Obeid R, Abi Younes N, Siddik M, Honein Abou Haidar G, Khoury FM.','2025','formalize aubmc generalizations skimming brainstorm quirkos familiarized rereading reconvened perianesthesia p23 p56 demeanor p52 p10 approachable ego smiled p23 welcoming skillful p33 p35 p29 quizzing p22 p55 methodical p36 p28 p52 debriefed p25 p18 p33 p27 p28 smoother cannulas p45 p33 p31 debriefings p33 shy judgmental judgmental p57 silly ore fromme respectfulness approachability patience debriefings deoliveira formalizing fullest supplmentary zmeo_a_2500555_sm6064 zmeo_a_2500555_sm6063 zmeo_a_2500555_sm6062 zmeo_a_2500555_sm6061 19kb carine zeeni halah mehp nathalie khoueiry zgheib elie saliba moukarzel reine obeid nadine younes midhat siddik gladys honein haidar msheik sproul seely eaglen lcme_color wipf aronowitz riveros catalani boggs toj toj mago jhs 0b013e8182fbdd bree whicker fromme fromme whicker paik hafler 544s 546s 544s gakhal gandell mededpublish negin daneshpayeh perel decastellarnau s1135 winn tews leering mendiola graue wiechers boker irby mededpublish lases arah pierik s0268 bucklin kvt bannister levett lathlean ascent belongingness hooven deci 02159x 02159x s63 mcclintock fainstad jauregui 11s 11s s46 s53 stalmeijer dolmans dhjm snellen balendong 0b013e1828fff12 supplmentary zmeo_a_2500555_sm6064 zmeo_a_2500555_sm6063 zmeo_a_2500555_sm6062 zmeo_a_2500555_sm6061 19kb','ABSTRACT Residents play a major role in medical student education during clinical clerkships. While various ‘Residents-as-Teachers’ (RAT) programs exist, few are tailored to anesthesiology, and little is known about students’ perceptions of effective teaching behaviors in this setting. This study aimed to evaluate the effectiveness of an educational workshop designed to improve the quality of resident teaching and to assess its impact on medical student learning outcomes and satisfaction. We also sought to identify specific resident teaching behaviors that students consider important for their learning and determine which of these behaviors improved post-intervention. The intervention targeted 1 st to 3 rd year clinical anesthesiology residents, and its effectiveness was evaluated through medical student feedback. We employed a pre-post-intervention design using a Quality of Supervision Questionnaire, with additional items on learning outcomes, career aspirations, and satisfaction. An open-ended question elicited students’ feedback on the behaviors of effective resident teachers. Data were analyzed using descriptive statistics and thematic analysis. Twenty-two residents participated in the intervention, and 132 medical students completed the questionnaires (77/106 pre, 55/58 post). Post-intervention, the mean score for the overall quality of teaching increased significantly (2.98 ± 0.53 pre vs. 3.40 ± 0.46 post, p < 0.001), with notable improvements across multiple domains. Student learning outcomes, interest in anesthesiology as a career, and rotation satisfaction improved significantly ( p < 0.001). The percentage of students identifying effective teaching behaviors increased from 63% to 96%. Thematic analysis revealed more prevalent teacher characteristics and enhanced teaching strategies with a direct positive impact on learning. Our study emphasizes the importance of formal training for anesthesiology residents to enhance teaching quality and student learning. Findings provide effective resident teaching strategies which educators can use to refine training programs in anesthesiology. Future research should explore structured evaluations of resident perspectives on the training program and explore broader implementation across different specialties and institutions. KEYWORDS: Teaching, learning, clerkship, anesthesiology, residents, students Introduction Residents play an important role in teaching medical students during clinical clerkships [ 1 , 2 ]. As such, the Liaison Committee on Medical Education (LCME) and the Accreditation Council of Graduate Medical Education (ACGME) mandate education training for resident teachers, which also helps prepare them to become future clinician-educators [ 3 , 4 ]. Residents-as-teachers (RAT) initiatives have been introduced to formalize this teaching role, providing structured training to enhance resident teaching skills [ 5 ]. Medical students’ experiential learning during the anesthesiology clerkship requires active teaching and supervision by faculty members and residents. A recent survey of 125 anesthesiology clerkship directors in the United States reported that 50% of student teaching is performed by faculty and 50% by residents [ 6 ]. The challenge, however, remains that anesthesiology residents rarely receive formal training or feedback on their teaching or supervision techniques, potentially limiting their effectiveness as educators [ 7 ]. Moreover, the field’s multidisciplinary nature requires students to interact with other specialties, including surgery, internal medicine, obstetrics and gynecology, emergency medicine, and critical care, in the distinctive setting of the (OR). An effective learning experience during this clerkship can help develop student interpersonal and communication skills, irrespective of future specialty, which can reduce medical errors and ultimately improve patient outcomes [ 8 ]. Additionally, knowledge and skills gained during an anesthesiology rotation, particularly the ABCs- airway, breathing, and circulation- are fundamental for all physicians. Therefore, exploring strategies to improve clinical teaching by anesthesiology residents for medical students is crucial. Several RAT programs have been described in the literature, primarily focusing on teaching skills, confidence, and knowledge in evidence-based medicine [ 9 ]. Although the benefits of these initiatives are well-documented, many residency programs still do not fully incorporate them into their curriculum [ 10 ]. This trend has been observed across several specialties, including internal medicine, geriatrics, and pediatrics [ 11–13 ]. There is a lack of published literature on a program specifically tailored to anesthesiology, with only a few small or pilot studies available [ 14 ]. Moreover, limited data exist on what specific teaching behaviors or strategies of anesthesia residents may improve because of such educational interventions. In this study, we sought to address these gaps by developing an educational workshop to enhance anesthesiology residents’ clinical teaching skills for medical students at a tertiary hospital. We aimed to evaluate the effectiveness of this workshop by evaluating medical students’ perceptions of its impact on resident teaching qualities, student self-perceived learning outcomes, career desire, and overall satisfaction. We also sought to identify specific anesthesia residents’ teaching behaviors that students consider important for their learning, and determine which of these behaviors improved post intervention. Methods Research design and study participants We employed a pre-post-intervention design to gather feedback from medical students on the effectiveness of an educational workshop using a 13-item quantitative survey. We included one additional qualitative open-ended question to gain a deeper understanding of which specific teaching practices of residents changed as a result of this intervention. We used a convenience sampling approach, inviting medical students who completed their anesthesiology clerkship 12 months before the intervention (a total of 106 between October 2022 to September 2023) and those scheduled to complete their clerkship post-intervention (a total of 58 between October 2023 to May 2024) to participate in the study. The intervention involved first-, second-, and third-year residents in the anesthesiology training program at the American University of Beirut Medical Center (AUBMC), a structured four-year competency-based training program that models United States residency structure and has received accreditation by the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education – International (ACGME-I). In October 2023, we conducted the educational workshop and invited all 23 residents to participate. Information about the workshop and the study was provided via email by an administrator who is not involved in the residency program or trainee assessment. Student and resident participation were voluntary, and no incentives were offered. All participants provided written informed consent. The study was approved by the Institutional Review Board of the American University of Beirut (approval number SBS-2023–0235). To ensure methodological rigor and transparency, this study adhered to the Strengthening the Reporting of Observational Studies in Epidemiology (STROBE) guidelines [ 15 ]. Theoretical framework Our research was guided by Kolb’s Experiential Learning Cycle theory, commonly depicted as a four-stage process, which ensures that students actively engage in an interconnected learning experience, with each stage reinforcing the next [ 16 ]. The theory posits that effective learning occurs when an individual progresses through all four stages: (1) concrete experience, (2) reflective observation, (3) abstract conceptualization, and (4) active experimentation. This model guided our educational intervention. Workshop participants were first exposed to simulated OR teaching scenarios, followed by observation and reflection on their experiences. This reflection aimed to develop new abstract concepts (analysis) and generalizations (conclusions) based on the simulated experience. Participants then applied the lessons learned to real-life teaching scenarios with medical students, creating new experiences in the workplace. Moving through this cycle is intended to foster increasingly complex and abstract ‘mental models’ related to teaching and supervising medical students. While this model is often depicted as a cycle, it is also widely used to describe preferred learning styles, where individuals may favor certain stages over others [ 17 ]. In our workshop, we aimed to design activities that engaged all four stages of experiential learning, ensuring that we accommodated variability in learning styles among our participants. Educational intervention This workshop was designed using Kern’s 6-step approach for curriculum development [ 18 ]. General and targeted needs assessment A survey conducted as part of our annual program evaluation revealed that the residents were eager to improve their teaching skills, while medical students valued practical application and interactive participation in anesthesia teaching. These findings suggested that our clerkship would benefit from structured educational interventions aimed at enhancing OR teaching and developing residents as educators. A targeted needs assessment, gathered through regular feedback meetings with third-year medical students at our institution, highlighted the need for more intraoperative teaching, hands-on experience, active participation in patient care, and real-time feedback during their anesthesiology clerkship. Anesthesiology is a unique specialty that requires high levels of procedural proficiency and real-time decision-making, where theoretical knowledge, technical skills, and professional attitudes are seamlessly integrated. Intervention goals and educational strategies Based on the needs assessment, the research team developed a workshop, with an outline summarized in Table 1 . Each module had specific goals and educational strategies, with the complete material available in the Supplementary Digital Content 1. Table 1. Workshop outline. Module Number Topic Objectives Time Module 1 Understanding the Clerkship Curriculum ▪ Develop an understanding of what we should be teaching medical students by skimming through the clerkship curriculum material 15 minutes Module 2 Understanding Important Teaching Behaviors for Residents ▪ Identify the challenges associated with effective students’ teaching ▪ Identify key teaching behaviors outlined in the Cleveland Clinic Teaching Tool and the De Oliveira Tool that residents can adopt to improve their effectiveness as educators in anesthesiology 10 minutes Module 3 Integrating Theory and Practice: how do teaching and learning happen? ▪ Investigate the mechanisms of student learning ▪ Apply Kolb’s Theory of Experiential Learning in the teaching of medical students ▪ Implement Miller’s Pyramid for Gradual Performance Assessment 20 minutes Module 4 Fostering Psychological Safety ▪ Define psychological safety in clinical learning environments ▪ Analyze the consequences of safe and unsafe learning environments ▪ Identify key behaviors fostering psychological safety ▪ Apply three psychological safety checklists in different scenarios in medical student education 20 minutes Module 5 Setting Clear Learning Goals ▪ Discuss the value and opportunities created by articulating clear goals and expectations for residents and learners ▪ Implement the practice of setting daily educational goals for medical students 10 minutes Module 6 Efficient Teaching Methods ▪ Differentiate between the traditional and One-Minute Preceptor teaching models ▪ Outline the five micro-skills of the One-Minute Preceptor model, explain the rationale for each step, and provide clinical teaching examples relevant to anesthesiology ▪ Apply the One-Minute Preceptor clinical teaching technique 15 minutes Open in a new tab Implementation We conducted a 90-minute, in-person workshop consisting of six modules that included both a theoretical component and small group workshop scenarios. The workshop was developed and delivered by an education faculty member, a clinician-educator, and a resident (a demographic overview of the workshop instructors is provided in Supplementary Digital Content 2). The intervention targeted 23 clinical anesthesia residents, from first- to third year, who are involved in teaching in the OR. This initiative represents the first step toward establishing a yearly curriculum designed to reinforce residents’ teaching skills, with plans for expansion based on future targeted needs assessments and data from this project. The workshop is practical to implement in a classroom setting with minimal resources (only printed checklists), offering benefits to students and residents without posing financial challenges. In modules three through six, participants were divided into groups of three, with one acting as the resident, another as the student, and the third as an observer. A distinct checklist was developed by the research team for each module. During all modules, facilitators rotated among groups, prompting residents to reflect on their teaching practices in real-time and adjust their approach as needed (reflection-in-action). After completing each scenario, participants engaged in structured debriefing sessions (reflection-on-action) to brainstorm the lessons they learned and how they planned to apply the knowledge and skills in the workplace. Evaluation and questionnaire development To evaluate the impact of the intervention on the quality of intraoperative teaching, we employed a pre-post assessment using both quantitative and qualitative measures targeting medical students and residents. Medical students’ perceptions of residents’ teaching skills This was assessed using the nine-item Quality of Supervision Questionnaire developed by De Oliveira Filho [ 9 ]. The instrument demonstrated internal consistency, as well as content and face validity, and had a single-factor structure [ 9 ]. It evaluates multiple dimensions of resident supervision and teaching effectiveness in the clinical setting, including perioperative planning, presence during critical moments, opportunities for procedural autonomy, availability, professionalism, patient-based learning stimulation, interpersonal skills, reinforcement of safety measures, and provision of timely feedback. Each question was rated on a 4-point scale (1 = Never, 2 = Rarely, 3 = Frequently, 4 = Always). Permission to use the instrument was obtained from the developer. Self-perceived learning outcomes, career aspirations, and overall satisfaction The research team developed four questions to gather feedback from medical students in these three domains. Learning outcomes focused on self-perceived knowledge acquisition and technical skill development, while career interest assessed whether the clerkship rotation increased students’ desire to pursue anesthesiology. Additionally, one item measured overall satisfaction with the rotation to explore whether improvements in resident teaching were associated with greater overall satisfaction with the clerkship experience. These questions were initially reviewed for content validity by three core clerkship faculty members and subsequently face-validated by two students not participating in the study. Each question was rated on a 5-point Likert scale (1 = Strongly disagree, 2 = Disagree, 3 = Neutral, 4 = Agree, 5 = Strongly agree). This scale is a good fit for bipolar constructs measuring agreement between two opposing directions (e.g., ‘strongly disagree’ to ‘strongly agree’) [ 19 ]. Resident teaching practices The research team created one open-ended question to prompt students to describe specific actions or behaviors by anesthesiology residents that they observed as effective in facilitating their learning during their clerkship. We also sought to evaluate the impact of the workshop on resident knowledge acquisition of clinical teaching concepts using pre-post questionnaires. The assessment consisted of 16 Likert-scale items (1 = not at all, 4 = to a great extent) developed by the workshop facilitators and covering key teaching concepts covered in the workshop material, including Kolb’s Theory of Experiential Learning and Miller’s Pyramid for performance assessment. The tools used are available within Supplementary Digital Content 3. Data analysis Descriptive statistics were used to interpret the students’ responses. Means (M), standard deviations (SD), and 95% confidence intervals (CI) were calculated for the medical students’ perceptions of the residents’ teaching skills and for each item on the satisfaction questionnaire. An independent sample t-test was used to identify differences between student responses before and after the intervention, while the Mann-Whitney U test was employed to compare resident responses. Statistical significance was defined as a p -value <0.05. For qualitative data, we conducted a pre-post inductive thematic analysis of medical students’ open-ended responses to identify resident teaching behaviors valued by students and to explore potential changes in reported behaviors post-intervention. The analysis was done with the support of Quirkos qualitative analysis software. One investigator (FM) conducted the initial coding, with the input of the senior researcher (GHA) throughout the process. The research team met regularly to discuss the analysis. First, the coder familiarized herself with the content of the open-ended responses, reading and rereading each response while taking notes on the data’s meaning. This was followed by identifying patterns and generating codes for each identified pattern. After coding 20 responses, she met with the senior researcher (GHA) to ensure consistency in the interpretation of meanings. Following this, FM independently coded the remaining transcripts. FM and GHA then reconvened to discuss the relationships between the codes and create a log of candidate themes. Common themes were selected, named, and defined. Discrepancies were reconciled through further review of the responses and discussion. Once the themes were generated, we used Bloom’s Taxonomy of learning domains to further categorize resident teaching strategies across cognitive, psychomotor, and affective domains. This approach helped structure the findings without imposing predefined categories on the initial coding process. Throughout the analysis, the researchers maintained reflexivity by keeping detailed reflective journals to document their research process and critically assess their dual roles as faculty members and investigators. This ongoing reflection ensured that coding decisions and interpretations remained objective and grounded in the data. Results Quantitative data Twenty-two residents were enrolled in the study and participated in the educational intervention. A total of 132 medical students completed the questionnaire, with 77/106 (72.6%) responding before the intervention and 55/58 (94.8%) responding post-intervention. There was a significant difference between the overall means for the quality of teaching as perceived by medical students before and after the educational intervention program (2.98 ± 0.53 vs. 3.40 ± 0.46, p < 0.001) ( Table 2 ). All aspects included in the quality of the teaching questionnaire showed significant improvement, except for the item regarding ‘residents were present during critical moments.’ Table 2. Overview of the quality of residents’ supervision as perceived by medical students. Supervisory Quality Before (n = 77) After (n = 55) MD 95% CI P value Mean SD Mean SD Perianesthesia Planning 2.88 0.79 3.15 0.71 −0.262 −0.527, 0.003 0.052 Presence during critical moments 3.60 0.54 3.73 0.49 −0.130 −0.312, 0.052 0.161 Opportunities for procedural autonomy 2.65 0.79 3.24 0.67 −0.587 −.0846, −0.328 <0.001 Availability 3.08 0.72 3.44 0.66 −0.358 −0.602, −0.115 0.004 Professionalism 3.18 0.72 3.49 0.57 −0.309 −0.541, −0.077 0.009 Stimulus to patient-based learning 2.61 0.85 3.25 0.67 −0.644 −0.916, −0.372 <0.001 Interpersonal Skills 3.12 0.61 3.45 0.63 −0.338 −0.553, −0.122 0.002 Reinforcement of safety measures 3.09 0.71 3.65 0.52 −0.564 −0.786, −0.341 <0.001 Provision of timely feedback 2.60 0.86 3.24 0.69 −0.639 −0.917, −0.361 <0.001 Overall Mean Score 2.98 0.53 3.40 0.46 −0.426 −0.599, −0.251 <0.001 Open in a new tab Scale: 1 = Never, 2 = Rarely, 3 = Frequently, 4 = always. SD: Standard Deviation. MD: Mean Difference. CI: Confidence Interval. Additionally, all aspects included in the Medical Students’ Self-perceived learning outcomes, career aspirations, and overall satisfaction with rotation showed significant improvement; i.e., enhancing knowledge, technical skills, interest in pursuing a career in anesthesia, and overall satisfaction ( p < 0.001 for all categories) ( Table 3 ). Table 3. Overview of medical students’ self-perceived learning outcomes, career aspirations, and overall satisfaction. Satisfaction with Anesthesia Rotation Before ( N = 77) After ( N = 55) MD 95% CI P value Mean SD Mean SD Self-perceived Knowledge Acquisition 3.65 0.93 4.29 0.63 −0.642 −0.927, −0.356 <0.001 Self-perceived Technical Skills Development 3.48 1.06 4.35 0.73 −0.865 −1.192, −0.538 <0.001 Career Interest in Anesthesia 3.18 0.98 3.73 0.89 −0.545 −0.876, −0.215 <0.001 Overall Rotation Satisfaction 3.45 1.08 4.25 0.73 −0.800 −1.132, −0.468 <0.001 Open in a new tab Scale: 1 = Strongly disagree, 2 = Disagree, 3 = Neutral, 4 = Agree, 5 = Strongly agree. SD: Standard Deviation. MD: Mean Difference. CI: Confidence Interval. Following the workshop, resident knowledge of key clinical teaching concepts, including Kolb’s Theory of Experiential Learning and Miller’s Pyramid showed significant improvement. Supplementary Digital Document 4 depicts resident pre-post scores. Qualitative data Participating students had the opportunity to describe the qualities and behaviors of anesthesiology resident teachers they found effective during their clerkship rotations. Before the intervention, 63% of participants (49 of 77) identified effective teaching behaviors and 96% (53 of 55) responded after the intervention. Inductive thematic analysis of student responses revealed the following themes: teacher characteristics and teaching strategies across the cognitive, affective, and psychomotor learning domains. Post-intervention, students identified several additional characteristics and strategies, suggesting an improvement in residents’ teaching practices. These themes are described below and summarized within a conceptual framework in Figure 1 . Figure 1. Open in a new tab Conceptual framework of anesthesia resident-to-student teaching. Teacher characteristics Students frequently mentioned specific characteristics that significantly impacted their learning experiences, especially after the intervention. The identified characteristics include: Commitment to teaching Before the intervention, students noted teaching qualities related to answering questions and sharing knowledge, with comments such as a resident’s ‘willingness to answer questions’ (G1.P11). Post-intervention, there was a shift toward more active teaching involvement. Students described residents as ‘eager to teach’ (G2.P14), demonstrating ‘a lot of passion and interest in the subject matter’ (G2.P2), and ‘ teaching in a proactive way’ (G2.P16). In addition, students observed that residents would ‘explain right away’ (G2.P23) as they entered the OR, showing a focus on teaching. The residents broadened students’ perspectives on anesthesiology, with one student stating that it ‘opened eyes to many aspects of surgery and anesthesia that were overlooked’ (G2.P6). This emergence of teaching characteristics post-intervention led to learning opportunities for students. Positive attitude Pre-intervention, students appreciated residents who displayed a ‘calm’ (G1.P56) and ‘helpful’ (G1.P53) demeanor, noting their accessibility and friendliness. Post-intervention comments highlighted a greater emphasis on additional characteristics. Students described residents as ‘kind’ (G2.P52), ‘respectful’ (G2.P4), and ‘patient’ (G2.P10), with one student noting that residents were ‘approachable without displaying any power dynamic or ego’ (G2.P2). This improvement in attitude was further reflected in simple gestures, such as a resident who ‘smiled as students entered the OR’ (G2.P23), fostering a welcoming environment conducive to learning. Knowledge and competence Before the intervention, students valued residents who were ‘clinically confident’ (G1.P53) and ‘skillful’ (G1.P33). Post-intervention, students identified a broader range of critical knowledge and competencies that improved their learning experience. They described residents as ‘well-informed’ (G2.P1), ‘knowledgeable’ (G2.P6), and ‘organized’ (G2.P35), while also highlighting their professionalism. Students commented on the residents’ ability to perform procedures with precision and confidence, noting their ‘professionalism in doing all the procedures’ (G2.P29). These qualities demonstrated an enhanced level of expertise post-intervention, positively impacting student learning. Teaching strategies Students identified various teaching strategies as particularly effective for anesthesiology instruction in the OR, with these strategies becoming more prominent post-intervention. They include: Teaching strategies: cognitive Students’ feedback highlighted several teaching strategies that enhanced knowledge acquisition and retention, promoted comprehension and critical thinking, and aided the understanding of complex concepts. These strategies were more commonly reported post-intervention, leading to clearer and more effective teaching practices. Knowledge assessment Before the intervention, there was little emphasis on structured knowledge assessments during resident-to-student interactions. Post-intervention, residents actively facilitated student-centered discussions, using quizzing techniques to gauge and expand student understanding of anesthesia-specific topics. One student noted that residents would now ‘prompt the student on what they know and whether they have prepared a specific topic, and then apply the topic to the case at hand’ (G2.P22). Another participant shared how this strategy challenged their medical knowledge: ‘One of the residents I worked with twice used to ask me what I knew about the case, and she tailored the discussion based on what I knew. She kept probing me to think and even asked me to find answers and discuss them the next day’ (G2.P55). This approach represented a clear improvement in fostering critical thinking compared to pre-intervention practices, where this strategy was less structured. In-depth explanation Before the intervention, students expressed a desire for more detailed and methodical explanations from residents. One student noted that they wanted residents to ‘explain the process in a step-by-step approach’ (G1.P36). There was a notable shift after the intervention, with residents now providing ‘detailed explanations’ (G2.P28) relevant to anesthesia, sharing their thought processes in real-time. Students appreciated how residents began ‘discussing the treatment plan and the basic knowledge behind every specific plan’ (G2.P52). Moreover, the frequency of post-case debriefing, which students had identified as a need before the intervention ( ‘I wished the resident debriefed after a case, especially if they were busy during the case’ (G1.P25)) became more prominent post-intervention. Students reported that residents took the time to explain procedures, even when they were unable to perform them: ‘For procedures that I couldn’t do, such as rapid sequence intubation, they would still explain everything afterward’ (G2.P18). Comprehensive teaching techniques and conceptual clarity Both pre-and post-intervention, students noted that residents employed algorithmic teaching and visual aids, with strategies like ‘basic algorithms to follow in specific anesthesia scenarios or situations’ (G1.P6) and ‘drawing on paper to explain anesthesia management’ (G1.P38). After the intervention, there was a marked improvement in how residents integrated evidence-based teaching. Students highlighted the inclusion of ‘recent research findings and guidelines, such as recent studies on perioperative pain management strategies or the latest recommendations for preventing postoperative nausea and vomiting’ (G2.P33). Additionally, intraoperative teaching became more focused and impactful, with residents creating ‘learning pearls during intraoperative time, spending the whole time between induction and extubation teaching students valuable concepts in anesthesia: fluid balance, induction agents, opioids, etc., all related to the case at hand’ (G2.P27). Engaging students in case-specific discussions, such as breaking down ‘every pharmacological agent’ (G2.P28), was highly valued and represented a notable improvement in teaching effectiveness. Teaching strategies: psychomotor Students highly valued teaching strategies that supported psychomotor learning, fostering clinical confidence, autonomy, and proficiency in specific tasks. These approaches also improved their efficiency and facilitated a smoother transition to hands-on tasks. These strategies included: Hands-on learning and practice Post-intervention, students noted that residents actively stepped back, providing more opportunities for hands-on participation, such as ‘getting involved in some of the procedures’ (G2.P6). This approach was particularly beneficial for developing skills in tasks like intravenous cannulas placement intubation, and ventilation. As one participant stated, ‘I believe that residents who allow students to perform procedures such as IV insertions and intubations are the best teachers because anesthesia is a highly practical field that requires a good deal of technical know-how’ (G2.P45). This increased autonomy post-intervention directly contributed to students’ enhanced clinical proficiency. Scaffolding Before the intervention, students often felt they lacked sufficient guidance when struggling with a procedure. Post-intervention, there was a noticeable improvement in the use of scaffolding as a teaching strategy. When students were unable to perform a procedure independently, residents were more likely to first demonstrate the technique and then allow students to attempt it under supervision. This approach was reinforced by real-time feedback and guidance. For example, during intubation supervision, residents provided ‘hands-on guidance, demonstrating proper technique while allowing students to perform the procedure under their supervision. They offered real-time feedback on hand positioning, blade manipulation, and airway visualization to enhance learning’ (G2.P33). This structured support became more prominent post-intervention, ensuring that students not only observed but also actively practiced critical procedures in a supportive learning environment. Teaching strategies: affective Post intervention, students highly valued additional teaching strategies that enhanced their sense of psychological safety in the clinical learning environment, making them feel more valued and respected. As a result, they maintained a positive energy, became more comfortable in the OR, and felt more motivated to learn. With increased confidence, they were able to ask questions without fear of judgment. This openly supportive and engaging atmosphere encouraged students to reflect on their actions and ‘learn from mistakes and build on correct work’ (G2.P2), ultimately boosting their confidence and enriching their overall learning experience. Inclusive participation and team integration Before the intervention, students often felt disconnected from the team, with limited opportunities for early engagement in patient care. Post-intervention, there was a notable improvement in how residents actively included students in the anesthetic process. Many students highlighted that residents now ‘cared about students being there and actively tried to make them part of the team’ (G2.P31). This included initiating pre-operative debriefings and ‘asking questions about the patient’s medical history, anticipated challenges, and anesthetic plan’ (G2.P33). Early engagement and integrating students as equal team members in discussions was perceived as highly effective. As one student noted, ‘Entering a room full of people we don’t know can sometimes be overwhelming for medical students, and we might shy away from asking questions. So, residents engaging us right away makes us feel more comfortable and increases our interest’ (G2.P16). This inclusive approach represented a shift from pre-intervention practices, where such active engagement was less common. Learning-focused and non-judgmental approach While residents made some efforts to normalize mistakes before the intervention, post-intervention strategies demonstrated a more explicit focus on learning and a non-judgmental approach. Residents became more proactive in reassuring students that ‘it is okay to make mistakes’ (G2.P57) and prioritized providing constructive feedback rather than assigning blame. One student appreciated that residents encouraged them to ask questions ‘even if the questions are silly’ (G2.P3), fostering a supportive environment where students felt comfortable expressing uncertainties and engaging ore actively in their learning. Discussion We designed and implemented an educational workshop to enhance the clinical teaching skills of anesthesiology residents and observed significant improvements in medical student perceptions of resident teaching. Student satisfaction increased across multiple dimensions, including knowledge acquisition, technical skill development, interest in pursuing a career in anesthesia, and overall rotation experience. Qualitative data provided deeper insights into specific teaching behaviors and strategies that facilitated student learning and became more prevalent after the intervention. Our findings align with previous research demonstrating that structured educational interventions can enhance residents’ confidence and competence in teaching medical students by providing explicit instructional strategies, feedback mechanisms, and opportunities for deliberate practice [ 20 , 21 , 22 ]. For example, Fromme et al. [ 11 ] found that residents participating in formal teaching workshops reported greater confidence in supervising students and improved self-perceived teaching efficacy. Similarly, a randomized trial by Morrison et al. demonstrated that structured RAT programs led to measurable improvements in residents’ ability to facilitate case-based discussions and provide formative feedback [ 23 ]. Our intervention significantly improved the perceived quality of resident teaching across nearly all student-evaluated aspects. One exception was resident presence during critical moments of the anesthetic procedure, which likely did not show significant improvement due to the inherently high level of resident presence in such situations, leaving limited room for measurable improvement. Our results also underscore the intervention’s effectiveness in promoting active learning, stimulating clinical reasoning, enhancing feedback, and fostering autonomy- key elements of clinical education. Increased resident availability to assist and problem solve, coupled with enhanced interpersonal communication skills, further illustrates how the intervention empowered residents to create a positive learning environment, ultimately boosting student confidence and competence [ 24 ]. Significant post-intervention improvements in students’ self-perceived knowledge and technical skill acquisition suggest that the intervention not only enhanced resident teaching but also facilitated student learning. Fundamental knowledge (ABCs) and skills (IV placement, intubation, and resuscitation) are multidisciplinary and essential for all physician, regardless of specialty. The improved resident teaching was further reflected in students’ increased interest in anesthesiology as a specialty, emphasizing the positive correlation between clerkship quality and career choice. This highlights the need for close attention to the impact of these clinical experiences. Qualitative findings revealed that desirable teacher characteristics like respectfulness, approachability, passion, patience, knowledge, and clinical competence became more apparent post-intervention, consistent with other studies [ 25–31 ]. While these qualities are generally challenging to cultivate through educational interventions, the workshop appears to have strengthened resident-student relationships, enabling residents to express these positive attributes more openly. The increased use of tailored teaching strategies, including case debriefings, baseline knowledge assessments, and explanations of management plans, significantly enhanced student learning experiences. These strategies align with problem-based learning (PBL) best practices, particularly those outlined in Malik & Malik’s Twelve Tips for Effective PBL Curriculum [ 32 ]. Malik and Malik recommend aligning teaching with modern learning theories by activating prior knowledge, building on existing knowledge, and consolidating new knowledge to help students see the relevance of their learning- strategies highlighted in our study that promote deep learning and critical thinking [ 32 ]. These strategies were developed among our residents through the workshop’s emphasis on bedside teaching tools, the One-Minute Preceptor model, and the application of teaching checklists (e.g., Cleveland Clinic and DeOliveira). Students also reported improved psychomotor learning through hands-on practice, guidance, and modeling, which are crucial for skill acquisition in anesthesiology [ 33 ]. This improvement can be attributed to the workshop modules focusing on Kolb’s Theory of Experiential Learning [ 16 ] and Miller’s Pyramid [ 34 ], along with the ‘Building Technical Skills’ checklist, which promotes support, participation, and empathy. Most importantly, the workshop’s emphasis on psychological safety contributed to residents creating a more inclusive environment [ 35 ]. Modules equipped residents with checklists for building a psychologically safe climate and reinforced the importance of open communication during case discussions. By applying these tools, residents encouraged student engagement, addressed knowledge gaps, and created a supportive learning atmosphere – essential for effective learning in the high-pressure context of anesthesiology. These strategies align with established clinical teaching models [ 35 ], particularly cognitive apprenticeship, which emphasizes modeling, a safe learning environment, coaching, knowledge articulation, and exploration as key teaching behaviors. Implications The implications of our study are multifaceted. From an educational perspective, it demonstrates that structured teaching interventions can enhance not only perceived teaching quality but also specific resident teaching behaviors. These behaviors, which were more prevalent post-intervention, offer valuable insights into how residency programs can better prepare residents as educators. Programs can leverage these findings by designing training modules explicitly targeting these teaching strategies. From a policy perspective, our results highlight the importance of formalizing resident training in behaviors that promote active learning and student engagement. Medical schools and residency programs should consider integrating these specific teaching behaviors into core competencies for resident educators, ensuring residents are not only clinically skilled but also competent teachers. Future research Future research should evaluate the long-term impact of educational interventions on resident teaching skills and student learning outcomes using objective measures like direct observations and performance assessments. Investigating various educational interventions to determine their most effective components would also be beneficial. Developing tailored training programs accommodating distinct experiential learner stage preferences and the specific needs of different residency programs and specialties could further enhance effectiveness and relevance. Future studies should also explore resident perspectives in greater depth to gain additional insights into improving OR bedside teaching for medical students. This should include structured evaluations of RAT programs using formal program evaluation frameworks, providing specific feedback to guide future program development and implementation in different contexts. Limitations Our findings should be considered in light of several limitations. First, the pre-post design does not account for potential confounding variables. Second, the single institution setting may limit the generalizability of the findings. Third, the resident and student sample size, while adequate for detecting medium to large effect sizes, may not be sufficient for smaller effect sizes. Also, self-reported questionnaires might introduce response bias. Future studies should consider a randomized controlled trial design, multiple institutions, and objective measures of teaching effectiveness. Further, while our content rich workshop provided scenarios grounded in theoretical frameworks and literature, it was not fully context rich. Simulated OR interactions, while designed to resemble real-world scenarios, were delivered in a controlled environment, not the dynamic, high-pressure OR. This limitation aligns with critiques of Kolb’s Experiential Learning Theory, which emphasize the importance of immersive experiences providing the fullest contextual information for effective experiential learning [ 17 ]. Future iterations should incorporate more immersive, authentic clinical teaching environments, such as direct student engagement in live OR cases, standardized patients, and real-time teaching assessments in clinical practice. Although we employed multiple types of data (quantitative assessments, open-ended question, and clerkship satisfaction ratings) to evaluate resident teaching behaviors, all responses were collected from students via a single survey, limiting methodological triangulation. Future studies could strengthen the validity by incorporating faculty observations of resident teaching and student interviews for a more comprehensive assessment. Finally, while we gathered informal resident feedback, this was not a study focus and we did not use a structured evaluation framework. This limited our ability to systematically assess resident perceptions of the training and its impact on their teaching confidence and learning. Conclusion Our study contributes to the literature by designing an educational intervention, guided by Kolb’s experiential learning cycle, that addresses the unique challenges of teaching in the high-pressure environment of anesthesiology. The findings highlight the importance of formal training for anesthesiology residents to enhance teaching quality, improve student learning, reduce the risk of negative clerkship experiences, and foster interest in the field. Supplementary Material Supplmentary Digital Content 1.pptx ZMEO_A_2500555_SM6064.pptx (1.7MB, pptx) Supplementary Digital Content 4.docx ZMEO_A_2500555_SM6063.docx (19.1KB, docx) Supplementary Digital Content 2.docx ZMEO_A_2500555_SM6062.docx (15.1KB, docx) Supplementary Digital Content 3.docx ZMEO_A_2500555_SM6061.docx (19KB, docx) Acknowledgments The authors would like to thank all residents and students who participated in this study. Funding Statement The author(s) reported there is no funding associated with the work featured in this article. Disclosure statement No potential conflict of interest was reported by the author(s). Author contribution Carine Zeeni, MD † and Halah Ibrahim † , MD, MEHP, the two authors equally contributed to the paper,