n of mechanically ventilated patients: a 1-day point-prevalence study in Germany. Crit Care Med. 2014;42:1178–1186. doi: 10.1097/CCM.0000000000000149. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 68. Berney SC, Harrold M, Webb SA, Seppelt I, Patman S, Thomas PJ, Denehy L. Intensive care unit mobility practices in Australia and New Zealand: a point prevalence study. Crit Care Resusc. 2013;15:260–265. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 69. Meriläinen M, Kyngäs H, Ala-Kokko T. Patients’ interactions in an intensive care unit and their memories of intensive care: a mixed method study. Intensive Crit Care Nurs. 2013;29:78–87. doi: 10.1016/j.iccn.2012.05.003. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 70. Happ MB, Garrett KL, Tate JA, DiVirgilio D, Houze MP, Demirci JR, George E, Sereika SM. Effect of a multi-level intervention on nurse–patient communication in the intensive care unit: results of the SPEACS trial. Heart Lung. 2014;43:89–98. doi: 10.1016/j.hrtlng.2013.11.010. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 71. Schwarzkopf D, Behrend S, Skupin H, Westermann I, Riedemann NC, Pfeifer R, Günther A, Witte OW, Reinhart K, Hartog CS. Family satisfaction in the intensive care unit: a quantitative and qualitative analysis. Intensive Care Med. 2013;39:1071–1079. doi: 10.1007/s00134-013-2862-7. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 72. Carrothers KM, Barr J, Spurlock B, Ridgely MS, Damberg CL, Ely EW. Contextual issues influencing implementation and outcomes associated with an integrated approach to managing pain, agitation, and delirium in adult ICUs. Crit Care Med. 2013;41(9 Suppl 1):S128–S135. doi: 10.1097/CCM.0b013e3182a2c2b1. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 73. Trogrlić Z, van der Jagt M, Bakker J, Balas MC, Ely EW, van der Voort PH, Ista E. A systematic review of implementation strategies for assessment, prevention, and management of ICU delirium and their effect on clinical outcomes. Crit Care. 2015;19:157. doi: 10.1186/s13054-015-0886-9. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 74. Walsh TS, Kydonaki K, Lee RJ, Everingham K, Antonelli J, Harkness RT, Cole S, Quasim T, Ruddy J, McDougall M, et al. Development of process control methodology for tracking the quality and safety of pain, agitation, and sedation management in critical care units. Crit Care Med. 2016;44:564–574. doi: 10.1097/CCM.0000000000001463. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',198,'2025-10-15 11:47:00','2025-10-15 11:47:00');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(6027,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12159245','Assessing chest radiographic quality and the influence of COVID-19 pathology: the Australian experience.','O''Reilly P, Abu Awwad D, Lewis S, Ekpo E, Reed W.','2025','scapulae costal slhd bsti 3mp 6mp glare 6mp 4mp ribs anteriorly posteriorly spinous clavicles scapulae bronchi costophrenic retrocardiac scapula scapulae scapula scapula retrocardiac ribs anteriorly posteriorly spinous clavicles scapulae bronchi costophrenic retrocardiac visualising straighten distorts contemporaneous scapulae stabilisation markings markings overexposure underexposure overexposure underexposure adaption erect immensely 4mp 6mp 4mp 3mp immensely graciously tompe sargar nbk564362 rolland unett campling royle broder masterclass radiologymasterclass chest_quality chest_xray_quality_projectin chieng radiopaedia radiopaedia oura honma kuwajima sugimori gallet hlady gumieri ddf hlabangana elsingergy sprmn europeanguidelineseur16260 muhogora nyanda kazema kjelle chilanga rejecting ryerson haramati fleischner balbi caroli corsi yousif komanduri qurashi rale obadia hajjam carlier yasin gouda rousan elobeid karrar khader jalaber lapotre morcet delattre ribet jouneau lederlin hyf aht e72 e78 moodley sewchuran swx tch e0245518 saade siblini karout decisiveness owusu banahene darko hasford asirifi medicalradiationpracticeboard kks abrahao obuchowski jcl krupinski kallergi cicchetti normed dasegowda ghanem cleverley afif razak choong s62 s69 masterclass masterclass gattinoni camporota tile radiopaedia radiopaedia masterclass masterclass radiopaedia radiopaedia','Abstract Introduction Quality assurance (QA) in medical imaging ensures consistently high‐quality images at acceptable radiation doses. However, the applicability of the chest X‐ray (CXR) QA tool in images with pathology, particularly infectious diseases like COVID‐19, has not been explored. This study examines the utility of the European Guidelines for image quality in QA of CXRs with varying severity and types of infectious disease. Methods A convenient sampling methodology was employed to recruit 25 participants (qualified radiographers: n = 13 and 4th‐year undergraduate radiography students: n = 12) to evaluate 70 CXR images using the European Guidelines for image quality in CXRs. The image dataset comprised of COVID‐19 and non‐COVID‐19 cases, which were randomly selected to reflect routine clinical practice variability. Participants independently rated image quality based on 10 criteria in the European guidelines on quality criteria for CXRs using a six‐point Likert scale. Image quality ratings of normal and pathological CXR images were compared using a Kruskal–Wallis test. Spearman''s ranked order correlation was used to assess the association between quality criteria ratings. Results CXRs with no pathology or non‐COVID pathologies exhibited statistically higher total QA scores compared to CXRs with COVID‐19 and indeterminate COVID‐19 ( P < 0.001). This trend was evident across various QA factors, especially those associated with patient inspiration. Higher levels of infection corresponded to lower QA ratings. No differences were found between different pathologies regarding the medial border of the scapulae being outside the lung fields. Conclusions Severe pathology negatively impacts the technical quality of CXRs with COVID‐19 features without necessarily affecting their diagnostic value. The findings emphasise the need to prioritise diagnostic ability over technical quality when evaluating CXRs exhibiting diffuse signs of pathology. Keywords: COVID‐19, CXR, diagnostic imaging, image quality and diagnostic value, image quality evaluation, medical imaging standards, radiographer performance, radiographic quality assurance, radiology education This study evaluates the impact of COVID‐19 pathology on the quality of chest X‐rays (CXRs) using an established European Guideline tool for image quality assessment. Results indicate that CXRs with COVID‐19 features have lower technical quality scores compared to non‐COVID‐19 images, though their diagnostic value remains unaffected. The findings suggest prioritising diagnostic ability over technical quality in CXRs with severe pathology. Introduction Accurate interpretation of radiographic images relies on the presence of a sufficient number of visual diagnostic features, providing certainty for radiologists and other reporting practitioners. 1 , 2 Radiographers play a pivotal role in attaining these radiographs of high quality by selecting appropriate exposure factors, ensuring correct patient positioning and using relevant technology. 3 Quality assurance (QA) programs, which evaluate diagnostic acceptability in radiography, are instrumental in establishing the quality of radiographs using different quality criteria. These criteria differ across body parts but emphasise the visualisation, reproduction, visually sharp reproduction and important image details that should be recognised. Additionally, physics‐based QA programs examine technical perspectives such as signal‐to‐noise ratios, collimation, pixel resolution and focal spot size. 1 , 3 , 4 , 5 The integration of these quality factors establishes the basis of an effective QA program to enable a measurable degree of conformity in each examination. 4 , 5 The ubiquitous chest radiograph (CXR) stands as the most common radiographic procedure, 6 , 7 with QA programs frequently implemented to maintain optimal standards for chest imaging. 8 QA for CXRs encompass a variety of criteria. These criteria include chest rotation, lung expansion, visibility of mediastinal structures, appearance of lower costal margins and artefacts, forming integral components of an effective CXR QA tool. 9 , 10 A seminal QA program for evaluating CXR quality, outlined in a 1996 European Union (EU) report, remains enduring. 11 The criteria in this report encompass image resolution of thoracic structures, levels of inspiration, levels of rotation, anatomical penetration and the appearances of superimposed anatomy. The continued use of the EU quality guidelines across numerous studies underscores their efficacy as a CXR QA tool, even in the digital era. 12 , 13 Given the global impact of the recent SARS‐CoV‐2 coronavirus pandemic (COVID‐19), CXRs have been recognised as a valuable tool for monitoring infection progression, severity, and prognosis. 14 , 15 , 16 , 17 , 18 With the substantial reliance on CXRs during the COVID‐19 pandemic, it is highly probable that CXRs will continue to be a crucial tool to assist diagnosis in future similar pandemics. COVID‐19 patients often present as extremely unwell, with multifocal and bilateral opacities and consolidations. 19 , 20 , 21 Many of these patients experience shortness of breath, presenting challenges for radiographers in terms of inspiration, positioning and maintaining infection prevention measures. 22 , 23 , 24 Additionally, patients requiring mobile CXRs are frequently connected to respirators and located in isolated rooms, requiring alternative positioning techniques. These factors challenge the acquisition of CXRs and should be considered when judging the quality of CXR images. Most radiographers often assess the quality of CXRs based on the typical technical criteria specified in the 1996 EU quality guidelines. 9 , 10 , 11 In many instances, images that do not meet these quality criteria are repeated even though they may be diagnostically acceptable by radiologists. 25 Radiographic repeats also increase the radiation dose burden and associated risk and limit the effective use of radiology resources. 26 Therefore, understanding the impact of patients'' health status on the technical aspects of imaging, the limits of technical image quality criteria and the relevance of diagnostic quality may be helpful in reducing repeat decisions. This should help radiographers understand the limits to which technical quality criteria can be applied when assessing the diagnostic utility of CXRs of severely ill patients. Hence, this study aims to assess the applicability of the EU quality criteria for the QA of CXRs with and without COVID‐19 infections and the extent to which pathology influences QA scores. Materials and Methods Ethics approval Ethics approval was obtained from the Sydney Local Health District (SLHD) Human Research Ethics Committee for both the use of CXR images and the overall study (protocol number 2020/3177). Participants Eligible participants comprised registered radiographers employed within the Sydney metropolitan area and final‐year diagnostic radiography (DR) students at the University of Sydney who had completed all placement requirements before graduation (a total of 44 weeks). This cohort of participants was selected because the evaluation of radiographic images using radiographic criteria is one of the skills and capabilities required for independent radiography practice according to domain 5a of the Medical Radiation Practice Board of Australia professional capabilities for medical radiation practice. 27 A convenient sampling methodology was used to recruit eligible participants. Recruitment was conducted through email invitations using the university platforms for students and clinical placement sites for qualified radiographers. Assessment scale The EU QA guidelines assessment scale was used to assess the quality of CXRs and was incorporated into the REDCap platform. The EU quality criteria is considered a reliable tool for assessing the quality of X‐ray images. REDCap is a secure web application specifically designed for creating and managing online surveys as well as storing data. 28 Each EU QA statement was linked to a Likert scale within the REDCap application, offering six possible ratings, ranging from ‘strongly agree’ (6 points) to ‘strongly disagree’ (1 point). The numerical scoring was structured so that a higher score indicated a higher level of technical quality for each CXR. Study cases A total of 70 CXRs were obtained from the tertiary referral hospital in the Sydney Local Health District, affiliated with the University of Sydney. The CXR dataset included images from four distinct categories: 30 CXRs from COVID‐19‐positive patients, 20 CXRs classified as indeterminate for COVID‐19 disease, 10 CXRs showing alternative chest pathology and 10 normal CXRs (Table 1 ). These cases were independently classified into these categories by two expert radiologists, suggesting perfect concordance between the radiologists. Table 1. Overview of the 70 CXRs. Disease categories Number of cases Male:female PA:AP Mobile vs. department CXR Characteristic for COVID‐19 30 14:16 5:25 24:6 Indeterminate for COVID‐19 20 9:11 1:19 2:18 Alternative 10 6:4 4:6 2:8 Normal 10 5:5 4:6 3:7 Open in a new tab AP, anterior to posterior projection; CXR, chest X‐ray; PA, posterior to anterior projection. The distributions of PA versus AP as well as mobile versus departmental CXRs in the dataset were to reflect the real‐world practice and the challenges of imaging severely ill COVID‐19 patients who often are imaged in the emergency department and may not be able to tolerate the standard PA projection. All 70 cases were selected from a diagnostic setting to ensure an even distribution of different disease features and to conform with the sample size and case distribution recommended for phase II observer/diagnostic performance studies. 29 The severity and type of the disease (COVID‐19, ‘indeterminate COVID‐19’, other chest pathologies and normal cases) were established independently by two radiologists who were not aware of their original diagnoses. All these cases were collected retrospectively, and all COVID‐19 positive cases were confirmed using the polymerase chain reaction (PCR) test as a reference standard. The four categories in the dataset were derived from guidelines proposed by the British Society of Thoracic Imaging (BSTI) for the reporting of COVID‐19 CXRs, which aims to enhance interobserver agreement, define similar infections and ensure accurate reporting of COVID‐19 CXRs. 30 Study design All CXRs in this study were viewed on monitors with a resolution of at least 4 M‐pixel (MP) monitors, although 3MP is the minimally acceptable level for reporting. 31 Participants had three options for viewing the cases. Firstly, participants were invited to use a viewing monitor of 6MP situated within a dedicated imaging research room located at the university. Ambient lighting was set to maximise imaging capabilities and reduce monitor glare. Participants were also allowed to use 6MP radiology monitors within their work department which were dedicated reporting rooms with optimum lighting conditions. Three participants accessed all 70 CXRs from their homes using personal computers, all with screen resolutions of 4MP. For each of the CXRs, participants were presented with a set of QA criteria based on the EU quality criteria for CXR images. To ensure that image quality assessment by radiographers using the EU quality tool can be relied upon to establish the applicability of the tool for QA of CXRs with and without COVID‐19 infections, the quality ratings of the first two radiographers who assessed the images were used to assess inter‐rater reliability. Inter‐rater reliability was calculated using the consistency model of the Intraclass correlation coefficient (ICC). Each participant independently reviewed the images and rated the CXRs using the EU criteria on the Likert scale. Prior to completing the study, each participant received instructions for using the EU quality criteria and the Likert scale for rating the quality of the images. Participants were also told to ignore pathology when evaluating the quality of the images, and there was no time limit for the radiographers to complete their ratings. Statistical analysis Non‐parametric tests were considered suitable for the analysis due to the use of the Likert scale. A Kruskal–Wallis test was used to compare the mean QA scores of the participants across all the categories of images in the dataset (COVID‐19, Indeterminate for COVID‐19, Alternative Chest Pathology and normal) for each EU QA criterion. A Spearman''s rank order correlation was used to assess the relationships between all QA variables. The Spearman''s rank order correlation was interpreted according to Cohen''s criteria 32 of 0–0.1 (very weak correlation), 0.1–0.3 (weak correlation), 0.3–0.5 (moderate correlation) and >0.05 (strong correlation). For all statistical analyses, the P ‐value was set as P < 0.05. Results A total of 25 participants completed the QA assessment of 70 CXRs. Reliability analysis yielded an ICC of 0.75, indicating good reliability. Participants were categorised into groups based on their period of qualification/experience in radiography. Details of participants are found in Table 2 . The average QA scores for qualified and student radiographers were 3.83 ± 1.62 and 3.92 ± 1.53 respectively. There were no statistical differences in the QA scores between the groups. Table 2. Participants experience categories and QA scores. Participating groups Qualifications (years'' experience) Number Quality assurance scores ± SD Qualified radiographers Greater than 15 years 4 3.98 ± 1.79 6–10 years 2 4.11 ± 1.03 0–5 years 7 3.66 ± 1.63 4th year undergraduate students CXR QA Interpretation part of studies 12 3.92 ± 1.53 Total 25 3.87 ± 1.58 Open in a new tab Overall, the average quality score for all 70 CXR images was 3.87 ± 1.58 out of a maximum score of six. The mean scores per QA factor and disease are presented in Table 3 . For nine of the 10 QA questions (excluding question three), the normal results category scored highest compared to the three alternative disease categories with an overall average of 4.56 ± 1.50. Additionally, the COVID‐19 positive disease category scored lower than both the indeterminate and alternative categories and significantly lower than the normal category across these QA questions with the lowest average of 3.63 ± 1.56. This difference in quality scores between reporting categories is statistically significant ( P < 0.001). Table 3. Mean scores per quality assurance factor and disease. QA questions COVID‐19 Indeterminate COVID‐19 Alternative chest pathology Normal Total average scores P ‐value Q1. Performed at full inspiration (as assessed by the position of the ribs above the diaphragm, either 6 anteriorly or 10 posteriorly) 3.72 ± 1.67 4.01 ± 1.63 4.77 ± 1.36 5.03 ± 1.31 4.14 ± 1.65 <0.001 Q2. Symmetrical reproduction of the thorax as shown by the central position of the spinous process between the medial ends of the clavicles 3.79 ± 1.53 4.07 ± 1.46 3.83 ± 1.43 4.63 ± 1.39 4.00 ± 1.51 0.010 Q3. Medial border of the scapulae to be outside the lung fields 2.35 ± 1.31 2.02 ± 1.11 2.88 ± 1.78 2.20 ± 1.29 2.31 ± 1.36 0.114 Q4. Reproduction of the whole rib cage above the diaphragm 3.24 ± 1.57 3.41 ± 1.60 4.03 ± 1.55 4.16 ± 1.58 3.53 ± 1.62 <0.001 Q5. Visually sharp reproduction of the vascular pattern in the whole lung, particularly the peripheral vessels 4.05 ± 1.39 4.18 ± 1.30 4.49 ± 1.35 5.08 ± 1.02 4.30 ± 1.36 <0.001 Q6. Visually sharp reproduction of the trachea and proximal bronchi 3.94 ± 1.45 3.83 ± 1.35 4.22 ± 1.24 4.55 ± 1.22 4.04 ± 1.38 0.025 Q7. Visually sharp reproduction of the borders of the heart and aorta 3.77 ± 1.44 4.10 ± 1.37 4.29 ± 1.38 5.08 ± 1.11 4.13 ± 1.43 <0.001 Q8. Visually sharp reproduction of the diaphragm and lateral costophrenic angles 4.02 ± 1.47 4.21 ± 1.35 4.34 ± 1.43 5.07 ± 1.16 4.27 ± 1.43 0.002 Q9. Visualisation of the retrocardiac lung and the mediastinum 3.87 ± 1.39 4.11 ± 1.35 4.37 ± 1.34 5.07 ± 1.09 4.18 ± 1.39 <0.001 Q10. Visualisation of the spine through the heart shadow 3.54 ± 1.61 3.47 ± 1.57 4.54 ± 1.29 4.74 ± 1.14 3.83 ± 1.58 <0.001 Total Scores 3.63 ± 1.56 3.74 ± 1.55 4.18 ± 1.51 4.56 ± 1.50 3.87 ± 1.58 <0.001 Open in a new tab The QA factor for the presence of the scapula border within the lung field, represented by question three, had the lowest average score of 2.31 ± 1.36, while question five, ‘visually sharp reproduction of the vascular pattern in the whole lung, particularly the peripheral vessels’, had the highest average score of 4.30 ± 1.36. Comparison of quality scores across CXR categories Comparing the total quality averages using the Kruskal–Wallis test, both normal and non‐COVID‐19 (alternative) pathologies in CXRs had statistically higher quality scores compared to CXRs with COVID‐19 and indeterminate COVID‐19 ( P < 0.001). Specifically, normal and alternative chest pathologies showed statistically higher quality scores compared to CXRs with COVID‐19 and indeterminate COVID‐19 for questions 1, 4 and 10 ( P < 0.001). Normal CXRs exhibited statistically higher quality scores compared to CXRs with COVID‐19, indeterminate COVID‐19 and alternative chest pathologies for questions 2 ( P < 0.001), 5 ( P = 0.017), 7 ( P < 0.001), 8 ( P = 0.002) and 9 ( P < 0.001). No differences were found between the different pathologies in terms of the medial border of the scapulae being outside the lung fields (question 3). Relationship between QA criteria Spearman''s Rank Order Correlation showed that the average total quality score is significantly correlated with all quality points except concerning the scapula border in the lung fields (question 3) (Table 4 ). The appearance of the scapula border in the lung fields showed insignificant and weak or negative correlations with other quality scores. Similarly, patient rotation (question 2) had a weak or moderate correlation with other quality assurance variables. All the visual quality questions (questions 5–10) had strong and significant alignment with one another. Inspiration (question 1) and reproduction of the whole rib cage above the diaphragm (question 4) were very strongly correlated (rho = 0.911, P < 0.01), and both had strong relationships with multiple exposure and visual factors, particularly regarding the visibility of the vascular pattern, heart borders and retrocardiac space (questions 5, 7 and 9 respectively). Table 4. Correlation between quality assurance factors. Questions Q1. Q2. Q3. Q4. Q5. Q6. Q7. Q8. Q9. Q10. Average quality score Q1. Performed at full inspiration (as assessed by the position of the ribs above the diaphragm, either 6 anteriorly or 10 posteriorly) 1 Q2. Symmetrical reproduction of the thorax as shown by the central position of the spinous process between the medial ends of the clavicles 0.301 * 1 Q3. Medial border of the scapulae to be outside the lung fields 0.019 −0.215 1 Q4. Reproduction of the whole rib cage above the diaphragm 0.911 ** 0.242 * 0.066 1 Q5. Visually sharp reproduction of the vascular pattern in the whole lung, particularly the peripheral vessels 0.677 ** 0.429 ** −0.004 0.615 ** 1 Q6. Visually sharp reproduction of the trachea and proximal bronchi 0.266 * 0.217 −0.254 * 0.252 * 0.616 ** 1 Q7. Visually sharp reproduction of the borders of the heart and aorta 0.631 ** 0.411 ** −0.204 0.635 ** 0.740 ** 0.546 ** 1 Q8. Visually sharp reproduction of the diaphragm and lateral costophrenic angles 0.440 ** 0.407 ** −0.367 ** 0.489 ** 0.615 ** 0.501 ** 0.685 ** 1 Q9. Visualisation of the retrocardiac lung and the mediastinum 0.633 ** 0.379 ** −0.282 * 0.625 ** 0.799 ** 0.616 ** 0.887 ** 0.826 ** 1 Q10. Visualisation of the spine through the heart shadow 0.498 ** 0.208 −0.156 0.524 ** 0.541 ** 0.553 ** 0.609 ** 0.580 ** 0.685 ** 1 Average Quality Score 0.786 ** 0.468 ** −0.061 0.781 ** 0.870 ** 0.603 ** 0.861 ** 0.758 ** 0.895 ** 0.764 ** 1 Open in a new tab * P < 0.05. ** P < 0.0. Discussion The results of this study show that pathology does have an association with quality and can be exacerbated by other relevant factors. Several factors influenced the relationship between the QA criteria and the four categories of chest X‐rays used in this study. The most prominent observation gleaned from the metrics used and outlined in Table 3 was the consistent high scoring of normal CXRs in every instance. Conversely, CXRs demonstrating COVID‐19 features scored the lowest in most of the QA criteria. The alternative disease category generally demonstrated scores closer to that of the normal chest X‐ray, while the indeterminate category exhibited scores closer to the positive COVID‐19 category. Further analysis showed that normal CXRs had higher QA ratings for criteria such as inspiration, the application of correct radiographic exposures to penetrate key anatomy, and the resolution of subtle lung anatomy. The quality of CXR images with alternative pathology had the second highest rating followed by images indeterminate for COVID‐19, with images of patients positive for COVID‐19 obtaining the lowest quality rating. In many clinical settings, especially during the pandemic, a significant proportion of patients were too unwell to be moved to a radiology department, necessitating the use of mobile X‐ray machines at the bedside. The use of mobile X‐ray equipment for hospitalised patients presents considerable challenges for radiographers. For instance, avoiding thoracic rotation is imperative for accurately visualising anatomical structures and interpreting CXR findings. Excessive rotation can result in distortion of the trachea, lungs, heart and other structures, complicating the assessment of their size and shape. 24 , 33 These findings are relevant to this cohort of patients, where the more unwell the patient, the more challenging it would be to ‘straighten up’ the thorax of the patient to minimise asymmetry. A CXR with asymmetry distorts the view of several intra‐thoracic anatomy, significantly reducing the quality of that image. 24 , 33 Moreover, patients with severe COVID‐19 symptoms would be even more problematic to correctly position due to decreased compliance as a result from the disease. 34 Further, increased workload, fatigue, personal dose exposure and limited space in rooms for mobile positioning are other challenges faced by radiographers for COVID‐19 patients 35 which may have contributed to the difference in scores between CXRs for patients with and without COVID‐19. The inclusion of CXRs acquired in both mobile and routine departmental settings in our dataset accounted for the different settings where CXR examinations of patients with COVID‐19 symptoms were performed. Furthermore, the inclusion of a larger sample of mobile CXRs ensured that our methodology reflected the real‐world conditions under which many of these images are taken and the actual conditions under which the EU QA tool would be used. Using only CXRs taken in a controlled radiology departmental setting may not capture the challenges and variability inherent in bedside imaging. Thus, inclusion criteria ensure that our findings can be generalised to contemporaneous acute clinical scenarios which were typical of many imaging services during the pandemic. However, this introduces a potential confounding factor, as the image quality of CXRs performed in a mobile setting may vary from a departmental X‐ray machine. This confounding effect must be considered when interpreting the results of the study. The position of the patient in the bed is another key factor. A majority of mobile CXRs were performed with patients placed in the typical anterior–posterior (AP) position. Examining patients in the AP position limits the ability to shift the medial border of the scapulae away from the lung fields. This limitation applied to all categories of CXRs with or without pathology when performed in the AP position and it was the only QA factor not influenced by the disease. The degree of rotation is another positional factor but remained influential between the different CXR pathology groups and applied to both AP and PA projections. The difference in results for rotation may be a result of limited equipment available to assist with positioning and stabilisation, splitting radiographers into ‘clean’ and ‘dirty’ roles which leaves one radiographer to position the patient, and differing patient cooperation and mobility depending on clinical symptoms. Thus, the presenting condition of the patient and challenges associated with radiographic examination of patients could explain the lower quality of images of those patients with pathology, particularly those with COVID‐19. The level of patient inspiration during the examination is another important factor to consider. Adequate inspiration ensures that the lungs are fully expanded, allowing for improved visualisation of lung parenchyma, airways, vascular markings, diaphragm and lung bases. Insufficient inspiration can result in poor definition of collapsed lung segments, obscured lung markings and difficulties in assessing the extent of infectious processes. 36 The QA factors related to inspiration found that CXRs showing no or non‐COVID pathologies had higher scores, whereas both COVID‐19 and indeterminate COVID‐19 had statistically lower scores. A recent study found that decreased levels of inspiration were synonymous with COVID‐19 patients and even found that ‘vigorous breathing efforts can amplify the severity of lung injury, which in turn can influence the duration of mechanical ventilation and impact patient outcome’ 37 Furthermore, proper exposure settings are crucial for achieving optimal image contrast, density and detail, enabling the visualisation of overlying structures such as those in the mediastinum. 38 , 39 Overexposure can result in low‐contrast images with obscured details, while underexposure can produce noisy images with poor visibility of anatomical structures. Both overexposure and underexposure can hinder accurate interpretation and potentially lead to misdiagnosis. 40 QA factors related to anatomy visualisation are heavily reliant on correct exposure techniques. For instance, question 10 relates to the visualisation of the spine through the mediastinum, which can at times visualise mediastinal pathologies. 41 , 42 A similar trend was observed with the normal category scoring the highest among these questions, followed by the alternative disease category, while the COVID category scoring the lowest. There are several factors outlined here that hinder a technically correct mobile examination of the COVID‐19 patient and lead to variable image quality. Lack of a grid, difficulties in positioning the unwell patient and lack of full inspiration can significantly hinder image quality. These factors can then exacerbate the problem by leading to other technical issues such as incorrect exposure settings and inappropriate source‐to‐detector distance. The findings indicate that CXRs of patients with severe COVID‐19 are often of poor technical quality. This highlights the challenges of obtaining acceptable images in a difficult clinical environment. These issues make it hard to use the EU guidelines'' technical quality criteria to determine the usefulness of suboptimal COVID‐19 CXRs for clinical decision‐making. It is also important to consider the context in which these images were taken and the clinical utility of these images, particularly in a pandemic situation like COVID‐19, where the priority is immediate results, even if the images are of suboptimal technical quality. It should be noted that patients with severe chest infections other than COVID‐19 present similar imaging challenges. However, the highly infectious nature of COVID‐19 and the need to immediately triage patients with severe respiratory symptoms should be considered when judging the quality of COVID‐19‐related images as acceptable or not. This does not imply that the EU quality guidelines have no utility in QA of COVID‐19 CXRs, but that there are reasonable limits to which technical image quality criteria can be used to establish the utility of CXRs for patients with severe COVID‐19. It is important to note that these CXRs were all accepted as reported diagnostic images and were chosen to reflect the real‐world environments in which they were taken. In spite of some technically poor‐quality images with severe respiratory disease, radiographers submitted these images and these images were accepted for reporting by radiologists. The findings highlight the adaption in current practice to accommodate the challenges of imaging patients with severe COVID‐19, and radiographers'' gradual shift in X‐ray image quality assessment in response to these challenges. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first study to examine the impact that severe COVID‐19 infections have on CXR image quality and the challenges in achieving the EU image quality benchmark for patients with severe respiratory infections. Our findings address a significant clinical knowledge gap in the evaluation of CXR quality in the acute setting using the EU quality criteria, particularly for patients with COVID‐19 chest infections. Another noteworthy finding was that the EU quality tool identified a difference between the quality of mobile CXRs and those of ambulant patients acquired in a conventional medical imaging department. Given the differences in image quality identified by the EU QA tool, it is important to establish variants of the tool to accommodate instances where these quality criteria cannot be met. This variant(s) should consider the health status of the patient. For example, in severe acute chest infections where imaging cannot be performed in the PA position, with the patient erect, on full inspiration and with the patient fully compliant with radiographic positioning, it may be challenging to acquire good technical quality CXR images. Therefore, refining the tool to accommodate instances where optimal imaging conditions cannot be achieved may be immensely beneficial in advancing radiographic practice. Limitations A potential limitation of our study is that we included three participants who evaluated the quality of the images using 4MP monitors while the remaining 22 participants completed the quality assessments using 6MP monitors in ideal reporting environments. However, it is important to note that 4MP monitors exceed the minimum acceptable monitor resolution or technical requirement (3MP) for viewing CXR images. It can be argued that the proportion of PA versus AP views and mobile versus departmental setting CXRs in our study could bias the results; however, it should be noted that these proportions capture the reality of current clinical practice. Thus, the study accounted for the clinical challenges of imaging severely ill COVID‐19 patients. Future studies should explore radiologists'' perception of image quality and benchmark radiologists'' image quality assessment against the EU quality criteria to establish the minimum acceptable standard for diagnostic utility of CXR images of patients with severe COVID‐19 infections. Finally, despite the 4th year students meeting all registration requirements and considered to have professional competencies to assess CXR image quality, they can be seen as a potential limitation. The same could be said for some of the qualified radiographers with less than 5 years of clinical experience. Recommendation A modified version of the EU QA guidelines specifically tailored for cases involving severe pathology, such as COVID‐19 should be developed. These adaptations should account for the practical challenges of imaging unwell patients, such as limited mobility and reduced compliance to better balance technical quality. The modified tool should allow radiographers to assess images based on their diagnostic value rather than technical quality. Targeted training for radiographers focusing on optimising image acquisition in challenging clinical environments, such as bedside imaging for critically ill patients, should be implemented. Training should emphasise strategies to achieve diagnostic‐quality images even when technical quality criteria cannot be fully met. Conclusion The presence of severe chest infections has a negative impact on the technical quality of CXRs without necessarily affecting their diagnostic utility. Progressively lower mean technical image quality scores were observed with increasing severity of COVID‐19 infection. While the EU quality tool is effective in distinguishing between poor and good technical quality images, it does not account for diagnostic image quality where good practice cannot be achieved due to patient condition and the imaging environment. Therefore, refining or adding variants to the EU CXR quality tool to accommodate acceptable diagnostic quality in instances where patient and imaging conditions cannot support the production of optimal technical quality images may be immensely beneficial in advancing radiographic practice. Conflict of Interest The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this article. Acknowledgements Thank you to all the radiographers and 4th‐year student radiographers who all graciously gave their time to participate in this study. Data Availability Statement The data that support the findings of this study are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request. References 1. Tompe A, Sargar K. X‐Ray Image Quality Assurance. [updated 2022 Oct 17]. In: StatPearls. Treasure Island (FL): StatPearls Publishing, 2023. Available from: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK564362/ . [ PubMed ] 2. Barrett HH, Yao J, Rolland JP, Myers KJ. Model observers for assessment of image quality. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA 1993; 90: 9758–9765. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Unett EM, Campling J, Royle AJ. Radiographic Techniques and Image Evaluation, 1st edn. Springer New York, NY: Springer, 1997. 10.1007/978-1-4899-2997-6. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Broder J. Imaging the chest: the chest radiograph. Diagnostic Imaging for the Emergency Physician, 2011; 185–296. 10.1016/B978-1-4160-6113-7.10005-5. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Radiology masterclass. Available from: https://www.radiologymasterclass.co.uk/tutorials/chest/chest_quality/chest_xray_quality_projectin . 6. Jones J, Chieng R, Murphy A, et al. Chest radiograph. Reference article, Radiopaedia.org (accessed on 04 Apr 2024) 10.53347/rID-14511; https://radiopaedia.org/articles/chest‐radiograph . [ DOI ] 7. Oura D, Sato S, Honma Y, Kuwajima S, Sugimori H. Quality assurance of chest X‐ray images with a 7. Combination of deep learning methods. Appl Sci 2023; 13: 2067. [ Google Scholar ] 8. Gallet JM, Reed MH, Hlady J. A novel quality assurance method in a university teaching paediatric radiology department. Br J Radiol 2000; 73: 843–846. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Gumieri DDF, Marques IS. Evaluation of chest X‐ray quality parameters. Int J Radiol Imaging Technol 2021; 7: 82. [ Google Scholar ] 10. Hlabangana LT, Elsingergy M, Ahmed A, et al. Inter‐rater reliability in quality assurance (QA) of paediatric chest X‐rays. J Med Imaging Radiat Sci 2021; 52: 427–434. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. European Commission . European Guidelines on Quality Criteria for Diagnostic Radiographic Images, 1996. Available from: https://www.sprmn.pt/pdf/EuropeanGuidelineseur16260.pdf . 12. Muhogora WE, Nyanda AM, Kazema RR. Experiences with the European guidelines on quality criteria for radiographic images in Tanzania. J Appl Clin Med Phys 2001; 2: 219–226. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Kjelle E, Chilanga C. The assessment of image quality and diagnostic value in X‐ray images: a survey on radiographers'' reasons for rejecting images. Insights Imaging 2022; 13: 36. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Rubin GD, Ryerson CJ, Haramati LB, et al. The role of chest imaging in patient management during the COVID‐19 pandemic: a multinational consensus statement from the